/* FP513 "why not the others?" rationales — generated by scripts/build_fp513.mjs from content/fp513/mN/why.json. */
window.MCQWHY = Object.assign(window.MCQWHY || {}, {"Under which arrangement does the investment banker guarantee the issuer the full proceeds and absorb the loss on any shares it cannot sell?":{"Best efforts underwriting":"Best efforts carries no guarantee; the issuer simply receives nothing for unsold shares.","Private placement":"A private placement is a sale to a small group of investors that skips SEC registration, not an underwriting guarantee.","Green shoe option":"A green shoe is only the right to increase the size of an offering."},"Which of these fictional clients qualifies as an accredited investor under Rule 501 of Regulation D?":{"Ivan, net worth of $1.5 million, of which $700,000 is equity in his primary residence":"Excluding his home, Ivan's net worth is $800,000, below the $1 million test.","Leah, single, income of $185,000 in each of the last two years and expecting a raise":"An individual needs income above $200,000 in each of the two most recent years.","A local charity whose total assets are $4 million":"A charitable organization needs assets exceeding $5 million."},"A dealer quotes Harbor Corp. at $18.40 bid and $18.55 ask. A client sells 200 shares to the dealer. Which statement is correct?":{"The client receives $18.55 a share, since a dealer buys at its ask":"The ask is the price at which the dealer sells, not buys.","The dealer is acting as the client's agent and earns a commission":"Acting as an agent for a commission describes a broker; a dealer trades as a principal for its own account.","The dealer is called a specialist because the trade is over the counter":"Over the counter the dealer is a market maker; a specialist is the dealer on a securities exchange."},"Which statements about venture capital are correct?<br>I. It typically finances privately held companies, often through convertible preferred stock.<br>II. It tends to have a low correlation with equities.<br>III. VC firms usually stay passive and avoid advising company management.<br>IV. Bridge financing is capital provided ahead of an expected IPO.":{"I and III only":"Statement III is false: VC firms generally provide management consultation and help with key hires.","II and IV only":"Statement I is also true: venture capital funds private firms, typically via convertible preferred stock.","I, II, III, and IV":"Statement III is false, so all four cannot be correct."},"Marcus, 58, has moderate risk tolerance, wants diversification and professional management, and may need his money within a few years. A friend pitches an oil-and-gas limited partnership. What is the best advice?":{"Buy the LP, since limited partners decide when distributions are paid":"The general partner, not the limited partners, controls distributions.","Buy the LP, since partnership income is passed through to him tax-free":"Partnership income is not tax-exempt; partners share in its income, gains, losses and deductions.","Join as the general partner, which limits his personal liability":"The general partner has unlimited liability; only limited partners have limited liability."},"Northgate Robotics sells new shares in its IPO at $20 each, paying underwriting costs of $1.40 a share. A week later, Kai buys 1,000 Northgate shares on the exchange at $26. How much of Kai's money goes to Northgate?":{"$26,000":"Kai bought previously issued shares from another investor in the secondary market, so the seller, not Northgate, receives the money.","$20,000":"$20 was the IPO price; that money reached Northgate only in the primary-market sale.","$18,600":"$18.60 a share (IPO price less underwriting costs) is what Northgate netted in the primary market, not from Kai's trade."},"Bianca sells short 300 shares of Lumen Co. at $60 with a 50% initial margin. Lumen pays a $0.80 per-share dividend before she covers, and she buys the shares back at $52. What is her percentage return?":{"26.67%":"This ignores the $240 dividend she owed the share lender ($2,400 &divide; $9,000).","12.00%":"This divides by the full $18,000 of sale proceeds instead of her $9,000 margin deposit.","29.33%":"This adds the dividend to her profit; a short seller pays the dividend, so it is subtracted."},"Elena buys 500 shares at $48 in her margin account with a 50% initial margin. Her broker's maintenance margin is 30%. At about what share price will she receive a margin call?":{"$24.00":"$24 is just the loan per share; equity would already be zero there.","$36.92":"This uses a 35% maintenance margin ($24 &divide; 0.65) instead of her broker's 30%.","$33.60":"This multiplies the price by (1 &minus; maintenance) instead of dividing the loan by it."},"Elena bought 500 shares at $48 on 50% initial margin, so she owes the broker $24 a share. The stock drops to $30 and the maintenance margin is 30%. How much cash must she deposit to restore the required equity?":{"$4,200":"This bases required equity on the $48 purchase price; the current $30 price must be used.","$4,500":"$9 a share is the total required equity, not the shortfall; her $6 of existing equity counts.","$3,000":"$3,000 is her actual equity ($6 &times; 500), not the amount she must add."},"Arjun buys 200 shares at $50 using 50% margin at 7% annual interest. During the year the stock pays a $1 per-share dividend, and he sells all shares at $58 at year-end. What is his holding period return?":{"18%":"18% is the unleveraged HPR ($1,800 &divide; $10,000); on margin, divide by his own $5,000.","36%":"This leaves out the $350 of margin interest, a negative cash flow.","25%":"This leaves out the $200 of dividends received, a positive cash flow."},"Noor owns 800 shares trading at $70. She wants out if the stock falls to $62, but will not accept any sale below $62, even if that means she may not get out. Which order fits?":{"Sell stop at $62":"Once triggered, a stop becomes a market order, which could fill well below $62 on a gap down.","Buy stop at $62":"A buy stop is placed above the market, typically to protect a short position.","Sell limit at $62":"With the stock at $70, a $62 sell limit would execute right away at $70 or better."},"Theo sold short 500 shares at $35 and wants to cap his loss if the stock rallies. Which order should he enter?":{"Sell stop at $30":"A sell stop below the market protects a long position, not a short one.","Buy limit at $30":"A buy limit below the market would only cover after the stock falls; it gives no protection in a rally.","Sell stop at $40":"Covering a short requires a buy; a sell stop is also placed below the market price."},"A brokerage firm holding a client's securities in street name goes bankrupt. Which statement about SIPC is correct?":{"It covers up to $250,000 per customer, all of which may be securities":"The limit is $500,000; $250,000 is the cap on the cash portion.","It covers market losses incurred while waiting for securities to be returned":"SIPC does not cover market losses suffered while waiting to recover securities from a failed firm.","It covers only certificates held by the client, not street-name holdings":"SIPC protects customer accounts held at the brokerage, which is exactly the street-name risk."},"Celia asks which of her holdings can be cashed in quickly at a known value but has no market where she could sell it to another investor. Which holding fits?":{"Her 26-week Treasury bill":"A T-bill is both liquid and marketable; it trades actively in the secondary market.","Her negotiable CD":"Negotiable CDs are bought and sold in the secondary market, so they are marketable.","Shares of a privately held company":"Private company stock is not liquid: it can't be turned into cash quickly at a known price."},"Owen buys a 26-week Treasury bill with a $10,000 face value for $9,880 and holds it to maturity. What is his holding period return, and how is the $120 taxed?":{"1.20%; ordinary income for federal tax only":"1.20% divides by the $10,000 face value; HPR divides by the $9,880 he paid.","1.21%; ordinary income for federal and state tax":"T-bill income is exempt from state and local income tax.","1.21%; long-term capital gain for federal tax":"Held to maturity, the discount is ordinary interest income, and the bill was held well under a year."},"Gwen is in a 24% federal and 6% state bracket (ignore any interaction between the two). She earns $500 of discount income on a T-bill and $500 of interest on a bank CD. How much more total income tax does the CD interest cost her than the T-bill income?":{"$0":"The two are not taxed the same: CD interest is state-taxable while T-bill income is not.","$120":"$120 is the federal tax on each; it is not the difference between them.","$150":"$150 is the CD's total tax ($500 &times; 30%), not the extra over the T-bill."},"A government securities dealer sells Treasuries to a money market fund for $4,990,000 and agrees to buy them back in 7 days for $5,000,000. What does the fund earn?":{"Nothing; the fund is the borrower and pays the dealer $10,000":"In a repo the dealer sells securities to raise cash, so the dealer is the borrower and the fund is the lender.","A $10,000 capital gain, because the deal is legally a sale":"Though legally a sale and repurchase, a repo is in substance a collateralized loan and the price gap is interest.","$10,000 of interest, about 0.20% per year":"The 0.20% is earned over just 7 days, not over a full year."},"Hal lives in a high-tax state and wants a money market fund whose income escapes both federal and his state's income tax. Which choice fits best?":{"A national tax-exempt fund, since all of its income is free of his state's tax":"A national fund's income is state-tax-free only for the portion from his home state's issuers.","A Treasury-only taxable money market fund, since its income is free of federal tax":"Treasury income is state-tax-exempt but fully subject to federal income tax.","A bank money market deposit account, since FDIC-insured interest is tax-exempt":"MMDA interest is taxable; FDIC insurance has no bearing on tax treatment."},"Why does commercial paper usually yield slightly more than a Treasury bill of similar maturity?":{"Its interest is exempt from state income tax":"Commercial paper interest is ordinary income; it is T-bill income that escapes state tax.","Its maturities commonly run up to two years":"Commercial paper matures in no more than 270 days.","It is secured by the issuer's receivables and inventory":"Commercial paper is unsecured; it is issued to finance receivables and inventory, not backed by them."},"A small U.S. importer can't get credit from its overseas supplier and is too small to issue commercial paper. Which money market instrument is built for this need?":{"Eurodollar CD":"A Eurodollar CD is a dollar deposit at a non-U.S. bank, an investment rather than trade financing.","Repurchase agreement":"Repos are used by government securities dealers to meet short-term liquidity needs.","Negotiable CD":"A negotiable CD is a large bank deposit bought mostly by institutions; it doesn't finance a firm's imports."},"Priya bought 200 shares of Delta Grid for $11,000. This year she sells all 200 for $8,200, and 18 days later she buys 200 Delta Grid shares for $8,600. What is her basis in the new shares?":{"$8,600":"This ignores the wash sale rule, which adds the $2,800 disallowed loss to the new basis.","$5,800":"The disallowed loss is added to, not subtracted from, the replacement cost.","$11,000":"The new basis starts from the replacement cost ($8,600), not the original cost."},"Rina bought 100 more shares of a stock on March 1. On March 21 she sells 100 older shares of the same stock at a $1,500 loss. What is the tax result?":{"A $1,500 loss is allowed because the purchase came before the sale":"The rule reaches purchases made within 30 days before the loss sale, not just after.","No loss now, and the $1,500 is permanently lost":"A wash sale postpones the loss by adding it to the replacement shares' basis; it isn't forfeited.","No loss now; the $1,500 reduces the basis of the March 1 shares":"The disallowed loss increases, not reduces, the replacement shares' basis."},"Leon has a $9,000 long-term gain, a $4,000 long-term loss, a $2,500 short-term gain and an $11,500 short-term loss this year. What is the result?":{"Deduct the full $4,000 net loss this year":"Net capital losses are deductible only up to $3,000 a year; the excess carries forward.","Deduct $3,000 this year and forfeit the other $1,000":"Unused capital losses carry forward indefinitely; they are not lost.","Deduct a $9,000 short-term loss and pay LT rates on $5,000":"After separate netting, the $5,000 net LT gain must be netted against the $9,000 net ST loss."},"Dmitri, a single filer, has MAGI of $230,000, including $45,000 of net investment income. Using the textbook's $200,000 threshold for single filers, what is his net investment income tax?":{"$1,710":"This taxes all $45,000 of investment income; the tax applies to the lesser amount, the $30,000 excess.","$0":"$0 would result from wrongly applying the $250,000 joint-filer threshold to a single filer.","$8,740":"The 3.8% applies to the lesser of NII or MAGI over the threshold, never to total MAGI."},"Which cash dividend is most likely to qualify for the preferential 0%/15%/20% rates?":{"From a REIT whose shares the client has held for three years":"REIT income isn't taxed at the entity level, so its dividends don't receive the preferential rates.","From an S corporation in which the client owns shares":"S corporation distributions aren't taxed at the corporate level first, so they don't get preferential rates.","From a C corporation stock bought 10 days before and sold 20 days after the ex-dividend date":"Held only about 30 days, the stock fails the more-than-60-days test within the 121-day window."},"Pia sells shares of a gold ETF she held for three years at a gain. What is the maximum federal rate on that long-term gain?":{"20%":"20% is the top rate for ordinary long-term capital gains, but gold and silver ETFs are generally taxed as collectibles.","25%":"25% is the maximum rate on the gain created by depreciation on real estate.","15%":"15% is the middle preferential rate, which doesn't apply to collectibles."},"Jae owns 100 shares with a $30 per-share basis and receives a $2 per-share distribution identified as a return of capital. What is the tax effect?":{"$200 taxed as a qualified dividend; basis stays $3,000":"A return of capital is not a taxable dividend; it gives back the investor's own money.","$200 taxed as ordinary income; basis falls to $2,800":"The distribution isn't taxed when received; it only lowers basis.","No current tax; his total basis rises to $3,200":"A return of capital reduces basis; it does not increase it."},"For a client who lives in State X, which statements are correct?<br>I. Interest on a U.S. Treasury note is federally taxable but exempt from state income tax.<br>II. Interest on a State X municipal bond is generally exempt from federal and State X income tax.<br>III. Interest on a State Y municipal bond is exempt from State X income tax.<br>IV. Interest on a corporate bond is taxable at both the federal and state level.":{"I and III only":"III is false: out-of-state municipal interest is taxable on the resident's state return.","II and III only":"III is false and I is true: Treasury interest is federally taxable but state-exempt.","I, II, III, and IV":"III is false, so not all four are correct."},"A bond's yield to maturity climbs from 4.10% to 5.35%. How large was the move in basis points?":{"12.5 basis points":"This treats a basis point as 1/10 of 1%; it is 1/100 of 1%.","1.25 basis points":"This simply copies the percentage-point change without converting it to basis points.","1,250 basis points":"This treats a basis point as 1/1,000 of 1%, overstating the move tenfold."},"Which of these ratings is the LOWEST that still counts as investment grade?":{"BB+ from Standard &amp; Poor's":"BB+ is the top of the noninvestment-grade (high-yield) range.","Ba from Moody's":"Ba is Moody's first speculative (high-yield) category.","A&minus; from Standard &amp; Poor's":"A&minus; is investment grade but sits above BBB&minus;, so it is not the lowest."},"Which risks does an investor holding U.S. Treasury notes face?<br>I. Interest rate risk<br>II. Reinvestment rate risk<br>III. Purchasing power risk<br>IV. Default risk":{"I and II only":"Fixed Treasury coupons and principal also lose purchasing power to inflation, so III belongs.","IV only":"Treasuries are treated as free of default risk; IV is the one risk they lack.","I, II, III, and IV":"Default risk does not apply; Treasuries are considered default-risk free."},"Market rates drop two full percentage points, yet Dana's corporate bond, callable at $1,040, barely trades above $1,040. What best explains the muted price gain?":{"Wider bid-ask spreads from liquidity risk are holding the price down":"Liquidity risk widens spreads on thinly traded issues; it does not create a ceiling at the call price.","Extension risk has lengthened the bond's expected life":"Extension risk arises when rates rise and mortgage prepayments slow, the opposite of this situation.","Lower rates raise purchasing power risk on the coupon":"Falling rates do not raise inflation risk, and inflation risk would not pin the price at $1,040."},"Marisol buys $50,000 par of a TIPS with a 2.0% coupon. The CPI rises 1.5% over the first six months. What is her first semiannual interest payment?":{"$500.00":"This ignores the CPI adjustment; the coupon is paid on the inflation-adjusted principal.","$1,015.00":"This applies the full annual 2% coupon to six months; each payment uses half the rate.","$761.25":"This uses the 1.5% inflation rate as the payment rate instead of half the 2% coupon."},"Theo bought a TIPS at its $1,000 par. This year the inflation adjustment raises the principal to $1,025, and he sells right afterward for $1,031. Ignoring coupon interest, how is the $31 above his cost treated?":{"$0 ordinary income; $31 capital gain":"The inflation step-up is taxed as ordinary income in the year it occurs, not as capital gain.","$25 ordinary income; $31 capital gain":"This double counts: the taxed $25 raises basis to $1,025, so the gain is only $6.","$31 ordinary income; $0 capital gain":"Only the $25 adjustment is ordinary income; the extra $6 is a sale gain."},"Which of the following securities carries the direct backing and guarantee of the U.S. government?":{"Federal National Mortgage Association (FNMA) securities":"Fannie Mae has only indirect government backing, so its issues pay slightly more.","Federal Home Loan Mortgage Corporation (FHLMC) securities":"Freddie Mac is a privately owned corporation with indirect backing, not a direct guarantee.","Student Loan Marketing Association (SLMA) securities":"Sallie Mae's securities have only indirect government backing."},"Rates rise sharply and homeowners stop refinancing, so principal on Victor's mortgage pass-through comes back far more slowly than projected. Which risk is he experiencing?":{"Prepayment risk":"Prepayment risk is the opposite case: falling rates speed up principal repayment.","Call risk":"Call risk involves an issuer redeeming a bond early, not cash flows arriving later.","Event risk":"Event risk is an unexpected shock such as fraud or a buyout, not a predictable slowdown in prepayments."},"Ruth, a cautious investor, wants the municipal bond with the least credit risk. Which should she choose?":{"A toll-bridge revenue bond":"Revenue bonds rely only on project income, so they are riskier than GO bonds.","An airport revenue bond":"An airport bond is repaid from facility revenues alone and is riskier than a GO bond.","A private activity bond financing a sports stadium":"Private activity bonds depend on a private business use and carry more credit risk than GO bonds."},"Owen faces a 35% marginal tax rate. A municipal bond yields 3.6%. What taxable equivalent yield must a corporate bond offer to match it?":{"2.34%":"This multiplies by (1 &minus; 0.35), which gives an after-tax yield, not a taxable equivalent.","4.86%":"This multiplies by 1.35 instead of dividing by (1 &minus; 0.35).","3.60%":"This ignores the tax advantage of the municipal bond entirely."},"Priya pays a 32% federal and a 6% state income tax (add them for simplicity). Which bond gives her the highest after-tax yield?":{"A corporate bond yielding 6.5%":"After 38% combined tax it nets 6.5% &times; 0.62 = 4.03%.","Her own state's municipal bond yielding 3.9%":"It is tax free at both levels but nets only 3.90%.","A U.S. Treasury note yielding 5.2%":"Free of state tax but federally taxable: 5.2% &times; 0.68 = 3.54%."},"Ken bought a corporate bond, originally issued at par, for $900 in the secondary market. When he sells it for $960, accrued market discount is $40 and he never elected to include it annually. How is his $60 gain taxed?":{"$60 capital gain":"Accrued market discount is taxed as interest income at disposition, not as capital gain.","$60 interest income":"Only the accrued market discount is interest; gain above it is capital gain.","$20 interest income and $40 capital gain":"This reverses the split; the $40 accrued discount is the interest portion."},"For which client would a taxable zero-coupon corporate bond held in a regular brokerage account be the POOREST fit?":{"Parents who need a lump sum for tuition in six years":"A zero maturing in six years matches a known sum on a known date.","A pension fund matching a payout due in 12 years":"A zero's duration equals its maturity, ideal for matching a single liability.","A speculator expecting rates to fall who wants maximum price gain":"Zeros are the most rate-sensitive bonds, so they suit a falling-rate bet."},"A Canadian bank issues U.S.-dollar bonds that are sold and traded in the United States. What are these bonds called?":{"Eurodollar bonds":"Eurodollar bonds are dollar bonds sold outside the U.S. and not SEC-registered.","Eurobonds":"A Eurobond is issued outside the home country of the currency it pays in.","Bulldog bonds":"Bulldog bonds are foreign bonds issued in the United Kingdom in pounds."},"A $1,000 par convertible bond has a conversion price of $80. The stock now trades at $96. What is the bond's conversion value?":{"$833.33":"This inverts the ratio (dividing par by the stock price, then multiplying by the conversion price).","$1,000.00":"This multiplies the 12.5 shares by the $80 conversion price instead of the $96 market price.","$1,152.00":"This drops the half share; the conversion ratio is 12.5, not 12."},"Using the facts shown, what is the bond's conversion premium as a percentage?":{"16.67%":"This divides the $180 premium by the bond's market price instead of its conversion value.","12.50%":"This is the investment premium ($120 &divide; $960), not the conversion premium.","11.11%":"This is the downside risk percentage ($120 &divide; $1,080)."},"Using the facts shown, what is the bond's investment premium as a percentage?":{"11.11%":"This divides by the market price; the investment premium is divided by investment value.","20.00%":"This is the conversion premium, measured against conversion value.","16.67%":"This uses the $180 conversion-premium dollars divided by market price."},"Using the facts shown, what is the bond's percentage downside risk?":{"12.50%":"This divides by investment value; downside risk is measured against today's market price.","20.00%":"This is the conversion premium, which has nothing to do with the bond floor.","16.67%":"This measures the fall to conversion value; the floor is investment value, not conversion value."},"Using the facts shown, what is the bond's investment (straight-bond) value?":{"$852.80":"This discounts annually; the exam default is semiannual (N = 20, I = 3%, PMT = $20).","$880.00":"This is the conversion value (16 shares &times; $55), not the straight-bond value.","$1,000.00":"Par equals value only when the coupon rate equals the market rate; here 4% &lt; 6%."},"Using the facts shown, what is the lowest price at which this convertible should trade?":{"$851.23":"Investment value is the floor only when it exceeds conversion value; here conversion value is higher.","$1,000.00":"Par is not a price floor for a convertible in the secondary market.","$1,731.23":"The floor is the greater of the two values, not their sum."},"Using the facts shown, suppose the convertible's market price is $1,000. What is its percentage downside risk?":{"17.48%":"This divides by investment value; downside risk is divided by market price.","13.64%":"This is the conversion premium ($120 &divide; $880), not downside risk.","12.00%":"This measures the drop to conversion value; the cushion is investment value."},"Lena's convertible bond has a conversion value of $1,150. The issuer announces it will call the bond at $1,040. What should she do?":{"Accept the $1,040 call price":"Accepting the call gives up $110 of value compared with converting.","Keep the bond and keep collecting coupons":"Once called, the bond stops paying interest; staying put is not an option.","Insist on repayment at the $1,000 par value":"The call price, not par, is what the issuer pays, and it is less than the stock value."},"Which statement about convertible bonds is CORRECT?":{"They usually pay higher coupons than comparable straight bonds":"Investors accept a lower coupon in exchange for the conversion option.","The issuer decides when they convert under normal terms":"Conversion is normally at the bondholder's option; only a call can force it.","Their prices are more volatile than the issuer's common stock":"Fixed coupons and a maturity date make them less volatile than the stock."},"Grace will need a lump sum in exactly six years and wants it immunized against rate changes. Which single holding best does that?":{"A 6-year Treasury with a 7% coupon":"A coupon bond's duration is shorter than six years, so it does not fully match the horizon.","A 4-year zero-coupon Treasury":"Its duration of four years falls short of the six-year horizon.","A bond fund with a six-year average maturity":"Average maturity is not duration, and a fund has no fixed maturity date."},"Why is an immunized bond portfolio not a 'set it and forget it' strategy?":{"Immunization only works while rates stay unchanged":"Immunization is built to offset rate changes, not to require stable rates.","Coupons must be spent rather than reinvested":"The offset relies on reinvesting coupons; spending them breaks it.","Duration must be kept double the time horizon":"Full offset requires duration equal to the horizon, not double it."},"Hector wants to lock in today's attractive long-term rates while keeping a large slice in short bonds, so he can redeploy cash if bond prices drop. Which structure fits?":{"A bullet":"A bullet concentrates maturities on a single target date.","A ladder":"A ladder spreads equal amounts evenly across all maturities, middle included.","An immunized portfolio":"Immunization matches duration to one horizon; it does not split holdings into short and long ends."},"Ivy needs a large sum in 10 years. She buys a 10-year bond now, an 8-year bond two years from now, and a 6-year bond four years from now. What strategy is this?":{"A ladder strategy":"A ladder staggers maturities; all of Ivy's bonds mature on the same date.","A barbell strategy":"A barbell pairs very short and very long bonds with nothing in the middle.","A rate anticipation swap":"A swap exchanges existing bonds on a rate forecast; Ivy is building toward a date."},"A bond manager expects interest rates to rise over the next year. Which swap is most consistent with that view?":{"Sell short-term bonds and buy long-term bonds":"That is the move for an expected rate decline; long bonds lose more when rates rise.","Sell a low-yield bond and buy a lower-quality, higher-yield bond":"That is a pure yield pickup swap, which is not based on a rate forecast.","Sell a government bond and buy a corporate bond":"That is an intermarket spread swap based on sector mispricing, not a rate outlook."},"Jada sold bonds she bought for $10,000 for $8,600 after rates rose, and reinvested the proceeds in similar bonds. Using the textbook's simplified approach (loss &times; marginal rate) with a 24% rate, what tax saving does this tax swap create?":{"$1,400":"This is the capital loss itself, not the tax it saves.","$2,064":"This applies the 24% rate to the $8,600 sale proceeds instead of the $1,400 loss.","$1,064":"This multiplies the loss by (1 &minus; 0.24) instead of by the tax rate."},"Without any interest rate forecast, a manager sells a 5-year A-rated bond yielding 4.6% and buys a 12-year BBB-rated bond yielding 5.4%. Which swap is this?":{"Substitution swap":"A substitution swap trades between bonds with identical features priced differently.","Rate anticipation swap":"A rate anticipation swap is driven by a rate forecast, which the manager does not have.","Tax swap":"A tax swap is motivated by realizing a loss for tax purposes."},"All else equal, which bond will show the greatest relative price change when market rates move?":{"5-year maturity, 2% coupon, 3% YTM":"A shorter maturity means smaller relative price swings.","20-year maturity, 7% coupon, 3% YTM":"A higher coupon dampens relative price swings.","20-year maturity, 2% coupon, 8% YTM":"A higher YTM dampens relative price swings."},"Jordan is aggressive and strongly expects interest rates to fall. Which bond portfolio best positions him to profit?":{"Short maturities with high coupons":"That is the defensive mix for expected rate increases.","Short maturities with low coupons":"Short maturities limit the price gain when rates fall.","Long maturities with high coupons":"High coupons reduce rate sensitivity and so the gain."},"Nadia lives in a state with a high income tax and wants taxable bonds with minimal default risk. Which is the best fit?":{"Federal National Mortgage Association (FNMA) debt":"Agency interest is generally taxable at the state level, unlike Treasuries.","High-yield corporate bonds":"High-yield bonds carry the most default risk of these choices.","Investment-grade corporate bonds":"Corporate interest is fully taxed by her state and carries some default risk."},"Walt and June, both 66, want inflation-adjusted cash each year of retirement with minimal default and call risk. Which approach does the textbook suggest?":{"A barbell of T-bills and 30-year corporate bonds in a taxable account":"Corporates add default and call risk, and neither end protects against inflation.","A single 30-year zero-coupon corporate bond":"One distant maturity gives no yearly cash and adds default risk.","High-yield bond funds held in a taxable account":"High-yield funds add default risk and offer no inflation adjustment."},"An inverted yield curve most often appears when":{"the economy is in a strong, early expansion":"Expansions usually produce a normal, upward-sloping curve.","the economy is peaking and no rate change is expected":"That describes a flat curve.","investors demand a liquidity premium for long maturities":"A liquidity premium produces an upward slope, not an inversion."},"The 1-year Treasury rate is 3.0%, and 1-year rates are expected to be 3.4% next year and 3.9% the year after. Under the unbiased expectations theory, what should the 3-year rate be today?":{"3.90%":"This uses only the last expected rate rather than averaging all three years.","10.66%":"This is the cumulative 3-year growth; it still needs the cube root.","3.00%":"This is just today's 1-year rate."},"Which yield curve theory can explain ONLY a normal, upward-sloping yield curve?":{"Unbiased expectations theory":"Expectations of falling or flat short rates let this theory explain any shape.","Market segmentation theory":"Independent supply and demand by segment lets this theory explain any shape.","Efficient market hypothesis":"The EMH is about stock price efficiency, not the term structure of rates."},"A life insurer buys only long-term bonds to match its long-dated policy liabilities and will not move into shorter maturities even when they yield more. Which theory does this behavior support?":{"Unbiased expectations theory":"That theory explains the curve through expected future short rates, not institutional maturity needs.","Liquidity preference theory":"That theory assumes investors prefer short maturities, the opposite of this insurer.","Rate anticipation theory":"Rate anticipation is a swap strategy, not a yield curve theory."},"The yield curve is inverted: 1-year paper yields 5.2% and 20-year bonds yield 4.4%. Lucia, an income investor, expects the curve to return to normal. What does the textbook suggest?":{"Buy 1-year paper for its higher yield":"Short paper matures and must be reinvested at lower rates once rates fall.","Avoid bonds entirely until the curve flattens":"This forgoes the high locked-in income and price gains as rates fall.","Buy long-term bonds that are callable soon":"Callable bonds would likely be called as rates fall, cutting off the gain."},"The curve is flat: 5-year and 30-year Treasuries both yield about 4.1%. Ben, an income investor, is thinking of moving from 5-year to 30-year bonds. What is the best advice?":{"Extend now, because the extra years add extra yield":"A flat curve pays no extra yield for the extra years of risk.","Extend now, because long rates cannot rise when the curve is flat":"Long rates can rise to restore a risk premium or as the whole curve shifts up.","Extend now, because a flat curve predicts a rate decline":"A flat curve signals little expected change in rates."},"Cobalt Corp. skipped its cumulative preferred dividends for the past two years. The board now wants to pay a common dividend. What must happen first?":{"Pay only the current year's preferred dividend":"Cumulative status requires the skipped prior-year dividends to be paid too.","Nothing; the skipped dividends are forfeited":"Forfeiture applies to noncumulative preferred, not cumulative.","File for bankruptcy over the missed dividends":"A missed preferred dividend does not trigger bankruptcy."},"A $50 par preferred stock pays a 5% dividend. Market yields on similar preferreds rise to 6.25%. What is its new intrinsic value, assuming no growth?":{"$62.50 (+25%)":"This divides by 4%, as if rates had fallen; rising rates lower the value.","$48.75 (&minus;2.5%)":"This simply cuts par by a small amount; value is dividend &divide; required return.","$50.00 (unchanged)":"A fixed dividend means price moves inversely with rates; it does not stay at par."},"Which of the following are similarities between bonds and preferred stock?<br>I. Holders generally have no voting power<br>II. Holders rank ahead of common stockholders in bankruptcy<br>III. The issuer may deduct the payments<br>IV. A missed payment triggers bankruptcy":{"I, II, and III only":"Preferred dividends are not tax deductible to the issuer.","III and IV only":"Both are differences: preferred dividends aren't deductible and missing one doesn't force bankruptcy.","I, II, III, and IV":"III and IV describe bonds only, not preferred stock."},"Which statement about convertible preferred stock is CORRECT?":{"Its dividend is usually lower than the common dividend":"The preferred dividend is generally higher than the common dividend.","Its conversion option loses value as the common stock rises":"The option gains value as the common stock price rises.","It ranks below common stock in a liquidation":"Preferred ranks ahead of common for dividends and in liquidation."},"From the issuing corporation's point of view, which statement best describes raising capital with common stock instead of bonds?":{"It avoids fixed payments, and dividends are tax-deductible":"Dividends paid on stock are not deductible to the corporation; only bond interest is.","It requires fixed payments, but dividends are tax-deductible":"This reverses both points: stock needs no fixed payments, and its dividends are not deductible.","It requires fixed payments, and interest is tax-deductible":"That describes debt financing, not equity."},"Rafael, age 61, plans to retire in two years and fears a recession. He wants to add one stock whose results depend least on the business cycle. Which is the best fit?":{"A national automaker":"Automakers are cyclical: profits rise in expansions and fall in downturns.","A casual-dining restaurant chain":"Restaurants are cyclical consumer discretionary businesses that suffer in recessions.","An emerging robotics developer":"An emerging growth/technology company carries high risk and is not defensive."},"Brightway Corp. pays for a large expansion by issuing new bonds rather than new shares. Which risk to Brightway's stockholders increases most directly?":{"Market risk":"Market risk comes from broad market moves, not from how this firm finances itself.","Business risk":"Business risk relates to the firm's operations and management, not its choice of debt over equity.","Interest rate risk":"Rate changes affect all stocks; the specific risk added by borrowing is financial (leverage) risk."},"Lena pays $2 per share for warrants that let her buy 80 shares of Corvex at $25 per share any time in the next four years. Corvex rises to $31 and she exercises. Ignoring commissions, what is her profit?":{"$160":"That is just the total cost of the warrants (80 &times; $2), not the profit.","$480":"This counts the $6 stock gain per share but forgets to subtract the $2 warrant cost.","$640":"This adds the warrant cost instead of subtracting it: ($6 + $2) &times; 80."},"Which feature is typical of a warrant but NOT of a subscription right?":{"An exercise price below the stock's market price at issue":"A below-market exercise price describes rights, not warrants.","A life of only a few weeks before it expires":"A very short life describes rights; warrants usually run for years.","An offer to existing holders to keep their ownership share":"Rights are offered to current shareholders to preserve their percentage ownership."},"Which statement correctly contrasts a warrant with an exchange-listed call option?":{"The warrant usually expires within nine months of issue":"Calls usually expire within nine months; warrants typically last several years.","The warrant's terms are standardized by the Options Clearing Corporation":"Standardized OCC terms describe listed calls; warrants are customized.","The warrant is written by an individual investor, not the company":"Calls are written by individuals; warrants are issued by the corporation."},"Which of the following defines the ex-dividend date?":{"The day the board of directors announces the dividend amount":"That is the declaration date.","The day the dividend is actually paid to shareholders":"That is the payment date.","The day the company's books are checked to list owners entitled to it":"That describes the record date, when settled owners appear on the books."},"Mateo wants to receive the next quarterly dividend on Halvern Corp. stock. Halvern's ex-dividend date is a Wednesday. Which purchase qualifies him for the dividend?":{"Buying on Wednesday at the market open":"Any purchase on the ex-dividend date comes without the dividend, even at the open.","Buying on Wednesday just before the close":"Shares bought on the ex-dividend date do not carry the dividend.","Buying on Thursday, the day after the ex-dividend date":"Purchases after the ex-dividend date are not entitled to the declared dividend."},"Tallis Inc. is worth $24 million and has 2 million shares outstanding. It declares a 20% stock dividend. What should the share price be afterward, all else equal?":{"$9.60":"This cuts the price by 20% ($12 &times; 0.80) instead of dividing value by the new share count.","$12.00":"The price cannot stay the same: the same value is now spread over 2.4 million shares.","$14.40":"A stock dividend lowers the price per share; it does not raise it by 20%."},"Priya owns 200 shares of Norland Co. that she bought at $45 each. The stock trades at $90 when Norland does a 3-for-2 split. Immediately afterward, what does she hold?":{"300 shares at $60; basis $45 per share":"Her total basis ($9,000) is now spread over 300 shares, so basis per share must fall.","400 shares at $45; basis $22.50 per share":"This treats the split as 2-for-1, not 3-for-2.","300 shares at $90; basis $30 per share":"The price must fall when shares increase; total value stays at $18,000."},"For a common-stock dividend to receive qualified (long-term capital gain) rates, how long must the shareholder have held the stock?":{"More than 12 months before the dividend payment date":"The 12-month rule is for long-term capital gains on sales, not the dividend holding test.","At least 30 days in the 61-day window centered on the record date":"This invents a window; the test uses 60 of 121 days measured from the ex-dividend date.","More than 60 days in the 121-day window starting on the record date":"The window starts 60 days before the ex-dividend date, not on the record date."},"Hal has $1,200 of dividends reinvested through his employer's DRIP this year. What is the tax result?":{"No tax is due until he sells the shares bought with the dividends":"Reinvested dividends are taxed in the year paid, just like cash dividends.","Tax is due on the $1,200 now, but his basis does not change":"Reinvested dividends are added to basis, which prevents double taxation when he sells.","No tax is due now, and his basis rises by $1,200":"Reinvestment doesn't defer tax; the dividend is taxable in the year paid."},"Corin Corp. pays a 10% stock dividend, and shareholders have no option to take cash instead. What is the federal income tax result for a shareholder?":{"Taxed now as a qualified dividend at the new shares' value":"A stock dividend with no cash option is generally not taxable when received.","Taxed now as ordinary income at the new shares' value":"No income is recognized; the holder owns the same share of the company.","No tax now; the new shares have a zero basis":"Basis is not zero; total basis is reallocated across all shares, lowering basis per share."},"The Aldergrove Fund holds securities worth $46.1 million and cash of $2.4 million, owes $1.3 million in liabilities, and has 2.36 million shares outstanding. What is its NAV per share?":{"$18.98":"This leaves out the $2.4 million of cash; total assets include everything the fund owns.","$19.53":"This uses only the securities and ignores both cash and liabilities.","$20.55":"This counts all assets but forgets to subtract the $1.3 million of liabilities."},"Claire enters an order at 11:15 a.m. ET to buy shares of an open-end mutual fund. What price will she pay, before any sales load?":{"The NAV computed at the prior day's close":"Mutual funds use forward pricing, not the last computed NAV.","The fund's NAV at the moment her order is entered":"Open-end funds don't price continuously; they price at the next calculation.","A market price set by supply and demand on an exchange":"That describes closed-end funds and ETFs, not open-end funds."},"Gwen, age 40, will put a large sum into one fund family and expects to hold it for more than 20 years. Which share class generally gives her the lowest total cost?":{"Class B shares with a contingent deferred sales charge":"B shares avoid an up-front load but generally cost more in total than A shares.","Class C shares with a level asset-based load":"C shares charge an ongoing 12b-1 sales fee every year, which adds up over long horizons.","Whichever class has the smallest up-front charge":"Total cost over the holding period matters, not just the initial charge."},"Teresa, an Oregon resident in a high tax bracket, wants bond fund income that is free of both federal and Oregon income tax. Which fund best fits?":{"A national municipal bond fund":"Most of a national fund's interest is subject to state tax; only the Oregon-issuer portion would be exempt.","A GNMA mortgage-backed securities fund":"GNMA fund income is federally taxable.","An investment-grade corporate bond fund":"Corporate bond interest is federally taxable."},"Nolan, an Ohio resident, received $2,400 of interest from a national municipal bond fund. The fund reports that 15% of its interest came from Ohio issuers. How much of Nolan's interest is generally exempt from Ohio income tax?":{"$0":"The fund reports the in-state share precisely so residents can exempt it from state tax.","$2,040":"That is the out-of-state portion (85%), which is subject to Ohio tax.","$2,400":"Only the share from Ohio issuers is state-exempt; a national fund is not fully exempt."},"What does a target-date (lifecycle) fund's glide path describe?":{"A fixed risk level the investor picks: conservative, moderate or growth":"Choosing a fixed risk level describes a lifestyle fund.","Moving up to 100% into stocks, bonds or cash based on forecasts":"That describes a flexible portfolio fund.","Holding about 60% stocks and 40% bonds at all times":"A steady 60/40 mix describes a typical balanced fund."},"Which feature distinguishes a closed-end fund from an open-end mutual fund?":{"It continuously issues new shares to meet investor demand":"Continuous issuance describes open-end funds; closed-end capital is fixed after the IPO.","Investors redeem their shares directly with the fund at NAV":"Closed-end shares are sold to other investors in the market, not redeemed with the fund.","Its share count rises and falls daily with purchases and redemptions":"A changing share count describes open-end funds."},"Which statement about unit investment trusts (UITs) is correct?":{"A board of directors oversees the trust's investment advisor":"UITs have no board, no corporate officers and no investment advisor.","The portfolio is actively traded in response to market changes":"UITs are passive: the portfolio is selected once and then held.","The trust continues indefinitely, like a mutual fund":"UITs have a limited, specified life and liquidate at the end of their term."},"Marcus has $600,000 to invest. He wants a professional manager, wants to exclude tobacco stocks, and wants control over when gains are realized on each holding. Which vehicle best fits?":{"A large-cap index mutual fund":"A fund holder owns a share of a pool, can't exclude holdings and has no lot-level basis.","A unit investment trust":"A UIT's portfolio is fixed at the start; it can't be customized or actively managed for taxes.","A closed-end fund":"A closed-end fund is still a pooled portfolio with no individual cost basis or exclusions."},"Which feature mainly makes ETFs more tax-efficient than most actively managed mutual funds?":{"A legal exemption from distributing capital gains":"ETFs have no such exemption; their structure simply tends to produce fewer gains.","They pay only qualified dividends":"Nothing restricts ETFs to qualified dividends; that depends on what they hold.","Forward pricing once at each day's close":"ETFs trade all day at market prices; forward pricing applies to mutual funds."},"Sofia wants low-cost, broad index exposure that she can trade during the day, buy on margin and sell short. Which investment best fits?":{"An S&amp;P 500 index mutual fund":"A mutual fund is priced once a day and can't be bought on margin or sold short like an exchange-traded share.","A stock unit investment trust":"A UIT has a fixed portfolio and isn't designed for intraday trading, margin and short sales.","A hedge fund of funds":"An FOF is costly, layered with fees, and not a low-cost index tool."},"Which client is the best candidate for a hedge fund investment?":{"Age 26, $40,000 net worth, wants long-term growth":"A hedge fund is beyond the means of a small investor seeking growth; a mutual fund fits better.","Retired widow, $300,000 net worth, needs monthly income":"She needs income and liquidity; hedge funds are for wealthy, sophisticated investors.","Couple saving for college in five years, $90,000 net worth":"Their modest assets and short horizon don't suit an illiquid, high-risk hedge fund."},"Which is a recognized drawback of a hedge fund of funds (FOF)?":{"It is open only to large institutions":"FOFs can actually give nonaccredited investors access to hedge funds.","Lower correlation with stocks than single hedge funds":"FOF returns have been more highly correlated with equity markets, not less.","All its assets sit in one hedge fund strategy":"An FOF spreads money across roughly 10&ndash;30 hedge funds for diversification."},"The trustee of the Pellham Co. 401(k) plan is choosing a GIC for the plan's stable-value option and expects market interest rates to rise during the contract term. Which choice is most prudent?":{"A nonparticipating GIC":"A fixed-rate nonparticipating GIC is better when rates are expected to fall.","A nonparticipating GIC with a longer term":"Locking a fixed rate for longer is the wrong move if rates are expected to rise.","A nonparticipating GIC with a shorter term":"It still pays a fixed rate; only a participating GIC's rate moves with market rates."},"Jun invests $3,000 a month in a stock fund. Over four months the share prices are $20, $15, $24 and $16. What is his average cost per share?":{"$16.00":"That is just the latest price, not the cost of all shares bought.","$18.75":"That is the simple average of the four prices (average price), not average cost.","$19.50":"That is the midpoint of the high ($24) and low ($15) prices."},"Ava uses value averaging with a target of +$2,000 in account value each month. In month 1 she invests $2,000 at $25 per share. In month 2 the price is $20. How much must she invest in month 2?":{"$1,600":"$1,600 is what her 80 shares are worth now, not the amount she needs to add.","$2,000":"Investing a fixed $2,000 is dollar cost averaging, not value averaging.","$4,000":"$4,000 is the month-2 target value, not the new money needed."},"Under Owen's value averaging plan, his account should be worth $6,000 at the end of month 3. He holds 250 shares, and the price is now $27. What does the plan call for?":{"Buy $750 of shares":"The account is above target, so value averaging requires selling, not buying.","Invest the usual $2,000":"A fixed monthly amount is dollar cost averaging; value averaging varies the amount.","Take no action until next month":"Value averaging adjusts every period to hit the target value."},"Imani buys 100 shares of a fund each quarter at prices of $40, $50 and $30. What are her total investment and average cost per share?":{"$12,000 invested; $38.30 per share":"$38.30 is what DCA-style weighting would give; with equal shares, cost equals the simple average price.","$15,000 invested; $50.00 per share":"This prices all 300 shares at the highest price.","$9,000 invested; $30.00 per share":"This prices all 300 shares at the lowest price."},"Which investor is best suited to share averaging rather than dollar cost averaging?":{"A value investor who wants more shares when prices fall":"Buying more shares on dips is what DCA does; it suits value-style investors.","A saver who needs the same dollar amount every month":"A fixed dollar amount is DCA; share averaging spends more when prices rise.","A hands-on investor who will buy heavily to hit value targets":"Buying to reach a target account value describes value averaging."},"Which statement about dollar cost averaging is correct?":{"It guarantees a profit if continued long enough":"DCA does not guarantee a profit, especially if the price keeps falling and never recovers.","It beats a lump sum in a steadily rising market":"In a steadily rising market, investing the lump sum up front buys more shares.","It requires selling when the account is above target":"Selling to hit a target is a feature of value averaging, not DCA."},"Dmitri bought shares of a fund in three lots: 200 at $10, 300 at $14 and 250 at $18. He now sells 200 shares at $20. Using the average cost method, what is his gain?":{"$400.00":"That is the specific identification result using the $18 lot.","$2,000.00":"That is the FIFO result using the $10 lot.","$4,000.00":"This treats the shares as having no basis at all."},"Rhea bought 500 fund shares for $9,750 plus a $250 commission. Later, $600 of taxable dividends were reinvested into 30 more shares. She now redeems all 530 shares at $23. What is her taxable gain?":{"$1,840":"This includes the reinvested dividends but leaves out the $250 commission.","$2,190":"This includes the commission but leaves out the $600 of reinvested dividends.","$2,440":"This leaves out both the commission and the reinvested dividends."},"A fund's management fee is 0.55%, its 12b-1 fee is 0.25% and its other expenses are 0.15%. It also charges a 4% front-end load, and its trading commissions equal 0.10% of assets. What is its expense ratio?":{"0.80%":"This leaves out the 0.15% of other expenses.","1.05%":"Trading commissions are not part of the expense ratio.","4.95%":"Sales loads are paid by the investor and are not in the expense ratio."},"Last year a fund bought $120 million and sold $90 million of securities. Its average monthly assets were $300 million. What was its portfolio turnover?":{"35%":"This averages purchases and sales; turnover uses the lesser of the two.","40%":"This uses purchases, the larger figure; turnover uses the lesser.","70%":"This adds purchases and sales together."},"Lucia puts $10,000 in each of two funds. Fund X gains 20% in year 1 and loses 10% in year 2. Fund Y gains 40% and then loses 25%. What are the ending values?":{"X: $11,000; Y: $11,500":"This adds the percentages instead of compounding them.","X: $10,800; Y: $11,500":"Fund Y must be compounded: $14,000 &times; 0.75 = $10,500.","X: $11,000; Y: $10,500":"Fund X must be compounded: $12,000 &times; 0.90 = $10,800."},"A fund's NAV falls 40% in a bear market. What percentage gain does it need to get back to its starting value?":{"40.00%":"The gain is earned on a smaller base, so it must exceed the 40% loss.","50.00%":"A 50% gain on 60% of the value only gets back to 90%.","60.00%":"A 60% gain on 60% of the value only gets back to 96%."},"Ava puts 30% of her portfolio in a fund she believes is 100% U.S. stocks. The manager has actually moved the fund to 75% stocks and 25% cash. What share of her total portfolio is really in stocks through this fund?":{"7.5%":"That is the cash portion (30% &times; 25%), not the stock portion.","25.0%":"This uses the fund's cash percentage instead of applying its stock percentage to her 30%.","30.0%":"This assumes the fund is fully invested in stocks, ignoring manager flexibility."},"In early December, Luis plans to invest $50,000 from a taxable account in a stock fund that had a strong year and is about to pay a large capital gain distribution. What is the best advice?":{"Buy now, since only long-time holders are taxed on distributions":"Distributions are taxed regardless of how long the buyer has owned the shares.","Buy now, since reinvested distributions are not taxed":"Reinvested distributions are taxable under constructive receipt.","Buy Class C shares, which skip capital gain distributions":"Share class doesn't change the fund's capital gain distributions."},"A fund family merges its worst-performing fund into a stronger fund, erasing the weak fund's record. What distortion in category return data does this create?":{"Survivorship bias that understates past category returns":"Dropping losers makes the remaining average look better, not worse.","Style drift that understates the category's risk":"Style drift is a fund changing its approach, not the removal of failed funds.","Forward pricing that inflates the surviving fund's NAV":"Forward pricing is how mutual fund orders are priced; it isn't a data bias."},"Which statement about portfolio turnover and tax efficiency is correct?":{"High turnover always leads to large capital gain distributions":"Realized losses can offset gains, so high turnover does not always produce big distributions.","Funds pass their net realized capital losses through to shareholders":"Funds don't distribute losses; they use them to offset gains.","Trading costs from turnover are included in the expense ratio":"Commissions, spreads and market-impact costs are outside the expense ratio."},"Which pairing of client objective and fund type follows this module's general suitability rules?":{"Maximum safety of principal &rarr; high-yield bond fund":"Safety of principal points to government bond funds; high-yield funds carry more default risk.","Aggressive growth &rarr; U.S. government bond fund":"Aggressive growth points to small-cap, technology or new-company stock funds.","High-bracket income seeker &rarr; corporate bond fund":"High-tax-bracket income seekers are directed to municipal bond funds."},"Corinne is looking at a 15-year, $1,000 par bond with a 4% coupon paid semiannually. Comparable bonds now yield 5.5%. What is the bond's intrinsic value?":{"$1,000.00":"Par value only results when the market yield equals the 4% coupon; here it is 5.5%.","$1,152.79":"This comes from entering 2.75 as I/YR with 2 P/YR, which halves the yield twice and produces a premium price.","$849.44":"This uses annual compounding (N = 15, PMT = 40); the coupon is paid semiannually."},"Ten-year, 6% semiannual-coupon bond; seven years remain until maturity. Comparable seven-year bonds yield 4.5%; ten-year bonds yield 5%. What should the bond sell for today?":{"$1,119.73":"This prices a full 10-year life at 4.5%; only 7 years of cash flows remain.","$1,041.66":"This uses the 3 years already elapsed as the remaining term.","$1,000.00":"Par would apply only if the market yield equaled the 6% coupon."},"A callable bond is trading above par. Which ordering of its yields is correct, from highest to lowest?":{"YTC, YTM, current yield":"This is the ranking for a discount bond, where accretion to par raises YTM and YTC.","YTM, current yield, YTC":"Current yield ignores the premium's erosion, so it sits above YTM for a premium bond.","All three are equal":"Equal yields occur only at par with no call premium."},"Paolo owns a callable corporate bond priced at $1,080. Which statements are correct?<br>I. Its current yield is above its YTM.<br>II. Its YTC is below its YTM.<br>III. Its coupon rate is below current market yields for comparable bonds.<br>IV. Being called would give him the lowest of the three yields.":{"I and II only":"Statement IV is also true: for a premium bond, being called produces the lowest yield.","III and IV only":"Statement III is false (a premium bond's coupon exceeds market yields) and I and II are both true.","I, II, III, and IV":"Statement III is false; a bond trades at a premium because its coupon is higher than market yields."},"Hal bought a 4% coupon bond at par. Comparable bonds now yield 5%. If Hal sells today, what best describes the sale?":{"He sells above par because rates rose":"Rising rates push prices down, not up.","He sells at par; the coupon resets to 5%":"A fixed-rate bond's coupon never resets; the price adjusts instead.","He sells at par because the coupon is fixed":"Because the coupon is fixed, the price must fall to deliver the higher market yield."},"Nadia's bond is priced at $980, has a duration of 6.2 (annual compounding), and the current YTM is 5%. If rates rise 75 basis points, what is the estimated new price using the textbook's duration formula?":{"$934.43":"This skips the (1 + y) divisor and uses &minus;6.2 &times; 0.0075 = &minus;4.65%.","$1,023.40":"This adds the change; rising rates lower bond prices.","$922.13":"This uses a full 1% rate change instead of 0.75%."},"A bond priced at $1,050 has a duration of 8.4 and a YTM of 6% (annual compounding). If rates fall 50 basis points, what is the approximate new price?":{"$1,008.40":"This subtracts the change; falling rates raise bond prices.","$1,094.10":"This omits the (1 + y) divisor: 8.4 &times; 0.005 = 4.2%.","$1,133.21":"This uses a 1% drop rather than 0.5%."},"Using the textbook's semiannual-compounding convention, estimate the new price of a $1,000 bond with a duration of 5.0 and a 7% YTM if rates rise 1%.":{"$953.27":"This is the annual-compounding answer (dividing by 1.07).","$950.00":"This is the rule-of-thumb estimate with no (1 + y) divisor.","$975.85":"This also halves the rate change; the textbook keeps &Delta;y at the full 0.01."},"A three-year, $1,000 bond pays a 6% annual coupon and yields 7%, so it is priced at $973.76. Using the cash-flow method, what is its duration?":{"3.00 years":"That is maturity; a coupon bond's duration is always shorter.","2.76 years":"This divides the time-weighted total by par ($1,000) instead of the price.","2.00 years":"This is a simple average of payment dates, ignoring present-value weights."},"All else equal, which bond has the highest duration?":{"20-year 6% coupon bond":"Its coupons pull duration below 20 years.","10-year zero-coupon bond":"Its duration is only 10 years.","20-year 8% coupon bond":"A higher coupon gives the lowest duration of the 20-year bonds."},"Ms. Okafor will need the money in 9 years. All four AAA bonds below are available. Which best immunizes her against interest-rate risk?":{"Bond A: 9-year maturity, duration 7.2":"It matches maturity to the horizon, but its 7.2 duration is well short of 9.","Bond C: 15-year maturity, duration 10.6":"A 10.6 duration overshoots the 9-year horizon more than Bond B.","Bond D: 10-year maturity, duration 8.0":"An 8.0 duration is further from 9 than Bond B's 9.1."},"An advisor strongly expects interest rates to drop over the next year. Which adjustment best positions a client's bond allocation?":{"Move into short-term bond funds":"Short-term funds have low durations and gain the least when rates fall.","Favor high-coupon, short-maturity bonds":"Both features lower duration, muting gains from falling rates.","Lower duration to match risk tolerance":"Nothing suggests a risk-tolerance problem; the rate outlook calls for longer duration."},"A short-term bond fund has a duration of 2.4 and yields 3.1%; a long-term fund has a duration of 12.9 and yields 4.4%. Using the rule of thumb, if rates jump 1%, about how many more percentage points would the long fund lose?":{"1.3":"That is the yield difference, not the difference in price loss.","15.3":"This adds the two durations instead of subtracting.","5.4":"This is the ratio of the durations (12.9 &divide; 2.4), not the gap in losses."},"A noncallable bond with positive convexity has a duration of 6. Rates rise 2.5%, so duration alone predicts a 15% price drop. What is the actual drop most likely to be?":{"Exactly 15%":"Duration is only accurate for small moves; at 2.5% the curve matters.","Somewhat more than 15%":"Larger-than-predicted losses describe negative convexity, not positive.","About 2.5%":"This confuses the rate change with the price change."},"Rates are expected to fall sharply. Which holding is most likely to gain LESS than its duration predicts?":{"A noncallable Treasury bond":"Straight (noncallable) bonds generally have positive convexity and gain more than duration predicts.","A long zero-coupon bond":"Long zeros have high positive convexity, amplifying gains when rates fall.","A low-coupon noncallable bond":"Low coupons raise positive convexity, which helps when rates fall."},"In the formula &Delta;P/P = &minus;D &times; [&Delta;y &divide; (1 + y)], what does y represent?":{"The bond's coupon rate":"The coupon is already built into duration; using it is a classic error.","The bond's current yield":"Current yield (coupon &divide; price) is a different measure from YTM.","The expected change in rates":"That is &Delta;y, the numerator."},"Which sequence describes top-down fundamental analysis?":{"Company, then industry, then economy":"That is bottom-up analysis.","Economy, then company, then industry":"Industry selection comes before screening individual companies.","Price trends, then volume, then company":"Price and volume study is technical, not fundamental, analysis."},"Brightwater Co. reports current assets of $450,000 (including $150,000 of inventory) and current liabilities of $300,000. What are its current ratio and quick ratio?":{"1.0 and 1.5":"The two ratios are reversed; removing inventory can only lower the ratio.","1.5 and 0.5":"This divides inventory by current liabilities for the quick ratio.","0.67 and 1.0":"This inverts the current ratio (liabilities over assets)."},"Which is a core assumption of technical analysis?":{"Only rational behavior drives security prices":"Technicians give irrational behavior (fear and greed) real weight.","Ratio analysis reveals a stock's true value":"That is fundamental analysis; technicians ignore ratios.","Prices instantly reflect all public news":"Instant adjustment contradicts the trend assumption; it is an efficient-market idea."},"The put-call ratio has spiked and mutual fund cash levels are unusually high. How would a contrarian read this?":{"Bearish, following the crowd":"Following the crowd is the opposite of a contrarian approach.","Bearish, since cash signals weakness":"High fund cash shows bearish managers, which a contrarian reads as bullish.","Neutral; contrarians use only odd-lots":"Odd-lot theory is one contrarian tool among several, including the put-call ratio and fund cash."},"The gap between high-quality and lower-quality bond yields in the Barron's confidence index has narrowed. What does this indicate?":{"A flight to quality; a bearish sign":"A flight to quality widens the spread, not narrows it.","Rising default rates; a bearish sign":"Default worries would push lower-grade yields up and widen the gap.","Nothing; it tracks only Treasuries":"The index compares high-grade and lower-grade bond yields, not just Treasuries."},"A client is convinced markets are semistrong-form efficient. According to the textbook, which form of analysis could help him beat the market?":{"Technical analysis only":"Technical analysis does not help in any form of the EMH.","Fundamental analysis only":"Fundamental analysis helps only if markets are merely weak-form efficient.","Both technical and fundamental":"Neither works under semistrong efficiency."},"Using fundamental analysis, an advisor concludes that Stock Q is undervalued after a steep drop. Which use of technical analysis does the textbook support?":{"Replace the valuation with chart signals":"The textbook keeps fundamental selection and uses technicals only for timing.","Sell because the trend is down":"The advisor already judged the stock cheap; technicals help time the purchase.","Ignore technicians; they cannot move prices":"The textbook says there are enough technicians to influence prices."},"Halcyon Corp. has total assets of $800 million, liabilities of $300 million, net income of $60 million and dividends of $24 million. What is Halcyon's ROE?":{"7.5%":"That is ROA (net income &divide; total assets).","7.2%":"That is the dividend growth rate (12% &times; 60% retention), not ROE.","20.0%":"This divides net income by liabilities instead of equity."},"Using Halcyon's figures (ROE 12%, net income $60 million, dividends $24 million), what is its sustainable dividend growth rate?":{"4.8%":"This multiplies ROE by the 40% payout ratio instead of the retention rate.","12.0%":"This is ROE alone; growth also depends on how much is retained.","4.5%":"This uses ROA (7.5%) instead of ROE."},"Two firms each raise $40 million. Firm X uses all equity; Firm Y uses $15 million of equity and $25 million of debt. Each earns $4 million (ignore interest). What are their ROEs?":{"X 10.0%, Y 10.0%":"This divides by total capital for both, which is a return on capital, not ROE.","X 10.0%, Y 16.0%":"This divides Y's earnings by its debt rather than its equity.","X 26.7%, Y 10.0%":"The results are swapped; the leveraged firm has the higher ROE."},"Juniper Inc. earns a steady 15% ROE. The board raises the dividend payout ratio from 30% to 60%. What happens to its sustainable growth rate?":{"Rises from 4.5% to 9.0%":"This multiplies ROE by the payout ratio instead of the retention rate.","Stays at 15.0%":"ROE alone is not the growth rate; retention matters.","Falls from 10.5% to 4.5%":"The new retention rate is 40%, giving 6.0%, not 4.5%."},"Firm L has a 40% ROE, a 6% ROA and heavy long-term debt. Firm U has a 22% ROE, an 11% ROA and no debt. Which is likely to hold up better in a business contraction?":{"Firm L, because its ROE is higher":"L's high ROE comes mostly from leverage, which adds risk in a downturn.","Firm L, because debt lifts its EPS":"Higher EPS from leverage comes with fixed interest that strains a contraction.","They should fare about the same":"Their capital structures differ sharply, and that drives downturn risk."},"A company's ROA is about one-seventh of its ROE. What does this most likely indicate?":{"The company has no debt":"With no debt, equity equals assets and ROE equals ROA.","The company pays no dividends":"Dividend policy does not set the ROA-to-ROE relationship.","The company's assets are undervalued":"The ROA/ROE gap points to capital structure, not asset valuation."},"A preferred stock pays a fixed $4.25 annual dividend with no maturity. Ellen requires an 8.5% return. What is the most she should pay?":{"$36.13":"This multiplies the dividend by 8.5 instead of dividing by 0.085.","$54.25":"This grows the dividend by r first ($4.25 &times; 1.085); a fixed dividend has no growth.","$4.61":"This is just the dividend grown by 8.5%, not a valuation."},"Arbor Co. just paid a $1.80 dividend, expected to grow 5% a year indefinitely. An investor requires 11%. What is the stock's intrinsic value?":{"$30.00":"This uses D<sub>0</sub> ($1.80) instead of next year's dividend.","$11.81":"This divides by r + g (0.16) instead of r &minus; g.","$37.80":"This divides by 0.05 (g) instead of r &minus; g (0.06)."},"Selma is offered Kestrel stock at $54. It just paid $2.40, dividends should grow 4% a year, and she requires 9%. What should she conclude?":{"Undervalued; worth about $49.92":"A price above intrinsic value means overvalued, not undervalued.","Overvalued; worth about $48.00":"$48.00 uses D<sub>0</sub> ($2.40 &divide; 0.05) without growing the dividend.","Undervalued; worth about $62.40":"This divides D<sub>1</sub> by g (0.04) instead of r &minus; g (0.05)."},"Wren Corp. just paid $1.40. Dividends should grow 8% a year for three years, then 3% indefinitely. The required return is 9%. Rounding each dividend to the cent, what is the intrinsic value?":{"$30.17":"That is the Year-3 terminal value, not discounted to today and without the early dividends.","$35.07":"This adds the dividends and terminal value without discounting.","$24.03":"This applies 3% growth from today and ignores the 8% stage."},"Pinecrest LLC pays no dividends. Its free cash flow to equity was $2.50 per share this year and should grow 4% a year. A client requires 12%. What is the intrinsic value per share?":{"$31.25":"This uses this year's FCFE without growing it one year.","$16.25":"This divides by r + g (0.16) instead of r &minus; g.","$20.83":"This treats FCFE as a no-growth perpetuity at 12%."},"Which model fits a profitable closely held company that has never paid a dividend and does not plan to?":{"Constant growth dividend discount model":"It requires a dividend stream growing at a constant rate.","No-growth dividend discount model":"It requires a fixed, ongoing dividend.","Multistage dividend discount model":"It still discounts dividends, just with changing growth rates."},"Which statements about the constant growth dividend discount model are correct?<br>I. The growth rate must be lower than the required return.<br>II. A higher required return lowers the stock's value.<br>III. It is best suited to companies in the growth phase.<br>IV. Next year's dividend equals D<sub>0</sub> &times; (1 + g).":{"I and III only":"Statement III is false and II and IV are true.","II and IV only":"Statement I is also true; the model breaks down if g is not below r.","I, II, III, and IV":"Statement III is false; growth-phase firms call for the multistage model."},"Larkspur stock trades at $63 with EPS of $4.20. The industry P/E is 18. Using the industry multiple, what is its estimated value, and what does it suggest?":{"$75.60; overvalued":"Value above market price means undervalued, not overvalued.","$52.50; overvalued":"This scales the price by 15/18 instead of multiplying EPS by the industry P/E.","$3.50; overvalued":"This divides price by the industry P/E, which is not a valuation."},"A client is choosing among four stocks. By the PEG ratio, which looks most attractive?":{"Alder: P/E 24, growth 20%":"Fast growth, but its PEG of 1.20 is above Cedar's 0.80.","Birch: P/E 15, growth 10%":"Its PEG is 1.50.","Dogwood: P/E 10, growth 5%":"It has the lowest P/E but the highest PEG (2.00) because growth is slow."},"A stock's earnings are expected to grow 6% and the investor requires 11%. Using the textbook formula, what is its price-to-free-cash-flow ratio?":{"20.0":"This leaves out the (1 + g) numerator.","6.24":"This divides by r + g (0.17).","9.64":"This divides 1.06 by r alone."},"Thistle stock trades at $42 with sales of $12 per share. Its peers average a P/S of 4.8. What is Thistle's P/S, and what does it suggest?":{"3.5; possibly overvalued":"A P/S below peers points to value, not overvaluation.","0.29; possibly undervalued":"This inverts the ratio (sales &divide; price).","4.8; fairly valued":"4.8 is the peer average, not Thistle's ratio."},"Which of these is a relative value method rather than an intrinsic value method?":{"Multistage dividend discount model":"It is an intrinsic value model.","No-growth dividend discount model":"It is an intrinsic value model.","Discounted free cash flow model":"It is an intrinsic value model."},"What is Harbor Point's annual net operating income (NOI)?":{"$77,000":"Subtracts both mortgage interest and depreciation, which are never part of NOI.","$97,000":"Subtracts mortgage interest; debt service reflects the investor's financing, not the property's cash flow.","$146,000":"Skips the 5% vacancy and collection allowance, overstating effective gross income."},"What is the building's estimated value, and how does the asking price compare?":{"$768,000; the asking price is too high":"Multiplies NOI by 8 instead of dividing by 0.08.","$1,200,000; the asking price is a bargain":"Value is correct, but paying $1,350,000 for a $1,200,000 property is overpaying.","$1,687,500; the asking price is a bargain":"Capitalizes effective gross income instead of NOI (expenses were never subtracted)."},"Which pair of requirements allows a REIT to avoid federal income tax at the entity level?":{"Distribute at least 75% of net annual earnings; derive at least 90% of gross income from real estate":"Swaps the two percentages.","Distribute all net annual earnings; derive all gross income from rents":"Overstates both tests; mortgage interest and other real estate income also count.","Distribute at least 90% of net annual earnings; hold only mortgage loans":"Equity REITs that own property qualify too; there is no mortgage-only requirement."},"Grace, 58, wants real estate exposure in her taxable account. She has $15,000, may need the money on short notice, and wants diversification across property types. Which choice best fits?":{"A nonpublicly traded real estate limited partnership":"RELPs are generally illiquid and usually require selling the whole interest.","A parcel of undeveloped land near a growing suburb":"Land is illiquid, produces no income, and is concentrated in one property.","The Z tranche of a collateralized mortgage obligation":"That is a mortgage debt instrument with the most interest rate risk, not property diversification."},"Which of the following statements about RELPs and REITs are CORRECT?<br>I. Income a REIT shareholder receives is portfolio (investment) income, not passive income.<br>II. A RELP investor generally must sell the entire partnership interest rather than part of it.<br>III. A REIT is managed by a general partner.<br>IV. Losses from a REIT are restricted by the passive activity rules.":{"I and III only":"III is wrong: a REIT is run by a board of directors; a RELP has the general partner.","II and IV only":"IV is wrong: REIT losses are capital losses outside the passive activity rules.","III and IV only":"Both describe a RELP, not a REIT."},"Lena wants to buy a CMO tranche but wants the least exposure to interest rate risk. Which tranche best fits?":{"Tranche Z, because its interest accrues like a zero-coupon bond":"Z is repaid last and carries the most interest rate risk.","A middle tranche, because risk is spread evenly":"Middle tranches wait for A to be retired, so they have longer repayment periods than A.","Any tranche, because each gets a pro rata share of principal":"Pro rata principal describes a pass-through MBS, not a CMO."},"Owen buys raw acreage, planning to sell once a highway extension is built. Which statement about his investment is CORRECT?":{"Appreciation is taxed each year as the land rises in value":"Appreciation is not recognized until a taxable sale.","The land produces positive cash flow from depreciation deductions":"Raw land produces no income and has ongoing expenses; it is held for appreciation.","Property taxes cannot be deducted until the land is sold":"Real estate taxes are normally deductible throughout ownership."},"Rivet Corp. stock trades at $62. A Rivet $55 call trades for $9.40. What are the call's intrinsic value and time value per share?":{"Intrinsic $9.40; time value $0":"Treats the whole premium as intrinsic; intrinsic value is only stock price minus strike.","Intrinsic $0; time value $9.40":"A call with the stock above the strike is in the money and has intrinsic value.","Intrinsic $7.00; time value $16.40":"Adds intrinsic value to the premium instead of subtracting it."},"Bexley stock trades at $48. Which option is in the money?":{"A $45 put":"A put with the stock above its strike is out of the money.","A $50 call":"A call with the stock below its strike is out of the money.","A $48 call":"Strike equals the stock price, so it is at the money with zero intrinsic value."},"Dana writes one uncovered put with a $60 strike and receives a $4 premium. What is her maximum possible loss on the contract?":{"$400":"That is her maximum gain (the premium kept if the put expires).","$6,000":"Ignores the $400 premium she received, which offsets part of the loss.","$6,400":"Adds the premium to the loss instead of subtracting it."},"Marcus buys one put on Orrin Co. with a $40 strike for a $2.50 premium. What is his maximum possible gain on the contract?":{"$250":"That is his maximum loss (the premium paid).","$4,000":"Ignores the $250 premium he paid.","Unlimited":"Unlimited gain belongs to a long call; a stock cannot fall below zero."},"Under the Black-Scholes model, holding everything else constant, which change DECREASES the value of a call option?":{"Higher volatility of the stock's returns":"More volatility raises a call's value.","A longer time until expiration":"More time raises a call's value.","A higher risk-free rate":"The risk-free rate is directly related to call value."},"Which statement correctly contrasts the binomial and Black-Scholes option pricing models?":{"Binomial assumes continuous price change; Black-Scholes assumes discrete up-or-down moves":"Reverses the two models' assumptions.","Black-Scholes values American puts; binomial values only European calls":"Black-Scholes is designed for European calls.","Black-Scholes ignores volatility; binomial is driven by volatility":"Volatility (&sigma;) is a core Black-Scholes input."},"Hal buys one call with a $50 strike for a $3 premium and later exercises it, buying 100 shares. What is his cost basis in the shares?":{"$4,700":"Subtracts the premium; for a call holder the premium is added to basis.","$5,000":"Ignores the premium, which becomes part of the cost of the shares.","$300":"That is only the premium; exercising also required paying the strike price."},"Priya wrote one call and received a $300 premium. The option expired unexercised. How is the $300 treated for tax?":{"Ordinary income when the premium is received":"The writer is not taxed on receipt; on expiration the premium becomes a short-term capital gain.","Long-term capital gain if the call was open over one year":"Expired premium is short-term for the writer regardless of how long the position was open.","No tax; the premium reduces the basis of her shares":"Basis adjustments apply when an option is exercised, not when it expires."},"Noor holds an American call with several months left. The call is in the money and she has decided to close the position. What is generally her better choice, and why?":{"Exercise the call, because exercise captures both intrinsic and time value":"Exercise captures only intrinsic value; time value is lost.","Exercise the call, because American options cannot be sold before expiration":"Listed options trade on exchanges and can be sold before expiration.","Let the call expire, because in-the-money calls lose value if closed early":"Letting it expire abandons both its intrinsic and time value."},"Ines owns 300 shares bought at $38. She writes 3 calls with a $45 strike for $1.80 each. At expiration the stock is $52 and the shares are called away. What is her total gain, ignoring costs?":{"$540":"Counts only the premium and ignores the gain from $38 to the $45 strike.","$2,100":"Counts only the stock gain to the strike and omits the premium.","$4,740":"Assumes she keeps the gain up to $52; above the $45 strike that upside belongs to the call buyer."},"Arjun owns shares now worth $80 and buys a $75 put for $3 to protect them until expiration. What is the most he can lose per share from today's value through expiration?":{"$2":"Subtracts the premium from the $5 gap instead of adding it.","$3":"Counts only the put premium and ignores the drop from $80 to the $75 floor.","$5":"Ignores the $3 paid for the put."},"Theo writes one naked call on Calder Co. with an $85 strike for a $2.40 premium. The stock jumps to $97 and the call is exercised. What is Theo's net loss, ignoring costs?":{"$240":"That is the premium he received, not his loss.","$1,200":"Ignores the $240 premium that partly offsets the loss.","$1,440":"Adds the premium to the loss instead of subtracting it."},"Elise buys a $60 call for $3.50 and a $60 put for $2.75 on the same stock, same expiration. At expiration, outside what price range does she profit?":{"Below $56.50 or above $63.50":"Uses only the call premium on each side instead of the combined $6.25.","Below $57.25 or above $62.75":"Uses only the put premium on each side instead of the combined $6.25.","Between $53.75 and $66.25":"Reverses the logic: inside that range the long straddle loses money."},"Ruth, a retiree, holds a large blue-chip position that she expects to trade sideways for months. She wants extra income and would accept selling at a modestly higher price. Which strategy fits best?":{"Buy a long straddle on the shares":"A long straddle needs a big move; sideways trading makes it lose both premiums.","Buy call options on the shares":"Buying calls costs premium and needs a rise; it produces no income.","Buy protective puts on the shares":"Puts cost premium and protect against declines; they do not add income."},"Felix sold short 500 shares of a stock he expects to fall. He worries about a sudden surge. Which option position best limits his risk?":{"Buy puts on the stock":"Puts gain when the stock falls, adding to the short bet instead of hedging it.","Write calls on the stock":"Writing calls adds more upside risk on top of the short sale.","Write puts on the stock":"Short puts lose when the stock falls and give little protection against a surge."},"Beatrix holds a single stock with a large gain. She wants downside protection without spending cash on premiums and accepts capping her upside. Which strategy fits?":{"Buy a put and buy a call on the shares":"Buying both options is a long straddle and costs two premiums.","Write a put and write a call on the shares":"That is a short straddle; it adds risk instead of protecting the gain.","Write a put and buy a call on the shares":"Writing a put increases downside exposure rather than protecting it."},"All else equal, which set of conditions lets a call writer collect the LARGEST premium?":{"Stock price below the strike, stable stock, long time to expiration":"An out-of-the-money call on a calm stock commands a small premium.","Stock price above the strike, stable stock, short time to expiration":"Low volatility and little time both shrink the premium.","Stock price below the strike, volatile stock, short time to expiration":"Being out of the money with little time left keeps the premium small."},"Walter's portfolio is mostly large-cap U.S. stock funds. He fears a broad market drop over the next few months but does not want to sell. Which hedge is most appropriate?":{"Write call options on the S&amp;P 500 Index":"Writing index calls adds income but offers only a premium-sized cushion against a big decline.","Buy call options on the S&amp;P 500 Index":"Calls profit when the market rises, not when it falls.","Buy puts on one stock in the largest fund":"A single-stock put does not match the broad portfolio's market movement."},"Corbin buys 2 corn futures contracts of 5,000 bushels each at $6.20 per bushel. The next day the price settles at $5.85. What happens to his account equity when it is marked to market?":{"It falls by $1,750":"Counts only one contract.","It rises by $3,500":"A long futures position loses when the price falls.","No change until the contracts are closed":"Futures are marked to market daily, so gains and losses hit equity each day."},"An investor is long 2 stock index futures contracts at 4,210. Each contract has a $250 multiplier. At settlement the index is 4,198. What is the investor's result?":{"Gain of $6,000":"A long position loses when the index falls from 4,210 to 4,198.","Loss of $3,000":"Counts only one of the two contracts.","Loss of $24":"Multiplies points by contracts but omits the $250 multiplier."},"Initial margin on a futures position is $5,000 and maintenance margin is $3,600. After losses, Rosa's equity is $3,200. How much must she deposit?":{"$400":"Restores equity only to the maintenance level; the call requires a return to initial margin.","$1,400":"That is just the gap between initial and maintenance margin.","$3,200":"That is her current equity, not the amount needed."},"A juice bottler will need to buy large amounts of oranges next season and fears prices will rise. How should it hedge with futures?":{"Sell orange futures, a short hedge":"Selling protects an owner against falling prices; the bottler is short the commodity.","Sell orange futures, a long hedge":"Mislabels the hedge, and selling would lose if prices rise.","No hedge; hedging is only needed when prices may fall":"A buyer who needs the commodity is hurt by rising prices, which a long hedge covers."},"Simone holds a large portfolio of long-term Treasury bonds. She expects interest rates to rise over the next quarter but wants to keep the bonds. Which futures position hedges her risk?":{"Buy Treasury bond futures":"Long bond futures lose when rates rise, adding to her bond losses.","Buy stock index futures":"Stock index futures do not track her interest rate exposure.","Sell stock index futures":"That hedges a stock portfolio, not a bond portfolio."},"Kofi held a futures contract for three months and had a $20,000 net gain at year end. How is the gain characterized for tax purposes?":{"$20,000 short-term":"Futures get 60/40 treatment regardless of the actual holding period.","$8,000 long-term and $12,000 short-term":"Reverses the split; 60% is long-term.","$20,000 ordinary income":"Futures gains are capital gains regardless of the underlying asset."},"Martina sells a rare coin collection held for nine years at a large gain. What is the maximum federal long-term capital gain rate on collectibles?":{"15%":"That is a common long-term rate for securities, not collectibles.","20%":"That is the top regular long-term rate for securities; collectibles have a higher cap.","37%":"Long-term collectibles gains are not taxed at the top ordinary rate."},"In which environment is gold most likely to be attractive to investors?":{"Real interest rates are high":"High real rates raise the opportunity cost of holding a non-income asset, the worst case for gold.","Inflation is falling and bond yields are rising":"That combination raises real rates, which works against gold.","Stock prices are rising steadily":"Gold often moves opposite to stocks, so a strong stock market is not its best setting."},"Harold, 70, has 40% of his net worth in rare stamps and needs more retirement income. What is the biggest planning concern with this allocation?":{"Stamp income is taxed at ordinary income rates each year":"Most tangibles produce no current income at all.","Stamps are highly correlated with his stock holdings":"Tangibles often have low or negative correlation with financial assets.","The stamps' basis will be stepped down at his death":"Appreciated tangibles get a step-up to fair market value at death."},"What is a major tax advantage of investing in natural resource programs such as oil and gas limited partnerships?":{"Gains are taxed at the 28% collectibles rate":"The 28% cap applies to collectibles, and it is not an advantage.","Income is exempt from federal tax while held":"There is no general exemption; the benefit is pass-through deductions.","Losses avoid capital loss limits by being treated as REIT losses":"REIT rules do not apply to natural resource programs."},"Which term describes the combined political and structural risks of investing in a particular nation, such as coups or confiscatory policies on dividends?":{"Exchange rate risk":"Currency risk is a separate risk arising from changes in exchange rates.","Liquidity risk":"Liquidity risk concerns how easily an asset can be sold at a fair price.","Reinvestment rate risk":"That concerns reinvesting cash flows at lower rates."},"Isabel's portfolio is already filled with U.S. large-cap and small-cap funds. She wants to diversify away from U.S. companies. Which fund type fits best?":{"A global fund":"Global funds also hold U.S. stocks, creating overlap with her domestic funds.","A U.S. large-cap index fund":"That adds more of the U.S. exposure she already has.","A single-country fund":"One country is concentrated and highly volatile, not a broad diversifier."},"Which statement about American depositary receipts (ADRs) is CORRECT?":{"Currency risk is eliminated because ADRs trade in dollars":"Dividends are declared in local currency and the price tracks the home market, so currency risk remains.","ADRs are issued by the foreign company's home government":"ADRs are trust receipts issued by a U.S. bank.","ADRs are illiquid because they trade only in the foreign market":"ADRs trade on U.S. exchanges and are relatively liquid."},"Which characteristic is LEAST typical of an emerging market?":{"Low levels of equity capitalization":"Small market capitalization is a typical emerging market trait.","Lower regulatory standards and less transparency":"Weak regulation is a typical emerging market trait.","Strong prospects for economic growth":"Growth potential is what attracts investors to emerging markets."},"What is her holding period return in U.S. dollars?":{"8.00%":"That is the return in euros; it ignores the currency move.","2.32%":"Assumes the dollar strengthened; here $1 buys fewer euros, so the dollar weakened.","13.88%":"Simply adds the 8% euro return and the 5.88% currency change; convert the actual cost and proceeds instead."},"What is his holding period return in U.S. dollars when the dollar strengthens to &euro;0.95?":{"8.00%":"Ignores the currency move; that is the euro return only.","14.35%":"Assumes $1 buys fewer euros at sale; here it buys more.","13.26%":"Treats the 5.26% currency move as a gain; a stronger dollar shrinks the dollar proceeds."},"The quote is $1.10 per euro. Wendell converts $3,300 into euros before a trip. How many euros does he receive, ignoring fees?":{"&euro;2,970":"Uses 0.90 as the inverse of 1.10; the true inverse is about 0.909.","&euro;3,300":"Ignores the exchange rate entirely.","&euro;3,630":"Multiplies by the dollars-per-euro rate instead of dividing."},"Hana, a Japanese investor, owns U.S. stocks. Holding stock prices constant, what currency move during the holding period helps her return measured in yen?":{"The yen strengthens against the dollar":"A stronger yen means her dollars convert into fewer yen at sale.","The dollar weakens against the yen":"That is the same as a stronger yen, which hurts her yen return.","Neither, because her stocks are priced in dollars":"Her return in yen depends on converting dollars back to yen."},"What happens to a futures contract's price as the contract approaches expiration?":{"It converges toward the daily price limit":"The daily limit caps one day's move; it is not a target price.","It moves away from the spot price as open interest falls":"Open interest counts outstanding contracts; it does not set convergence.","It locks at the initial margin level":"Margin is a deposit, not a price the contract converges to."},"The dollar currently buys 0.92 euros. If the U.S. dollar is revalued (strengthens), which quote could result?":{"$1 buys 0.89 euros":"Fewer euros per dollar means the dollar weakened (devalued).","One euro costs $1.12":"Since 1 &divide; 0.92 &asymp; $1.087, a higher dollar cost per euro means the dollar weakened.","$1 still buys 0.92 euros, but euro goods cost more":"A revaluation changes the exchange rate itself."},"Which statement best distinguishes asset allocation from diversification?":{"Asset allocation and diversification are two names for the same portfolio process.":"The textbook says diversification is not a synonym for asset allocation.","Diversification is only achieved once a portfolio holds all four broad asset classes.":"Diversification can occur within one class, such as a dozen stocks across industries.","Holding a dozen stocks in varied industries is asset allocation rather than diversification.":"That is diversification within one asset class (common stock), not allocation across classes."},"For CFP exam purposes, how many large-cap stocks in different industries does the textbook treat as enough for a diversified portfolio?":{"4 to 7 stocks":"That range applies to mutual funds in different asset classes.","25 to 30 stocks":"That range is the rule of thumb for mid- or small-cap stocks.","About 100 stocks":"Some studies suggest up to 100, but the exam rule is 10 to 15 large caps."},"Dalia, 34, and her father Ramon, 67, are each considering the same aggressive growth fund whose annual returns swing widely. Why can Dalia hold it more safely?":{"Younger investors are not exposed to systematic risk.":"Systematic risk affects every investor regardless of age.","The fund's standard deviation is lower for a younger shareholder.":"A fund's volatility is a property of the fund, not of who owns it.","Her portfolio beta automatically falls because of her age.":"Beta depends on the holdings and their market sensitivity, not the owner's age."},"A client is choosing between a Treasury bond fund expected to return 6% and a speculative overseas mining venture expected to return 8%. What is the best advice?":{"Pick the mining venture because its expected return is higher.":"Judging by return alone ignores the risk taken to earn it.","Pick the Treasury fund because lower risk is always the better choice.":"Looking only at risk is also wrong; too little risk may not meet goals.","Pick the mining venture because more risk guarantees a higher realized return.":"Higher risk raises the return investors demand; it guarantees nothing."},"Which of the following is a systematic (nondiversifiable) risk?":{"Business risk":"Business risk is firm- or sector-specific and diversifiable.","Financial risk":"Financial risk comes from one firm's capital structure and is diversifiable.","Marketability risk":"Marketability risk is listed among the unsystematic risks."},"Which of the following are unsystematic risks?<br>I. Default risk<br>II. Purchasing power risk<br>III. Investment manager risk<br>IV. Tax risk":{"I and III only":"Tax risk (IV) is also on the textbook's unsystematic list.","II and IV only":"Purchasing power risk (II) is systematic, and I and III are omitted.","I, II, III, and IV":"Purchasing power risk (II) is the P in PRIME, a systematic risk."},"Corwin owns common stock in a privately held regional bakery chain. Which risk does he NOT face as a shareholder?":{"Liquidity risk":"A private company's shares may take a long time to sell, so liquidity risk applies.","Business risk":"The chain's operating income is uncertain, so business risk applies.","Market risk":"Market risk is systematic and affects essentially all investments."},"Priya, retired, wants a bond holding that eliminates reinvestment rate risk. Which should she consider?":{"A GNMA mortgage-backed security":"GNMAs return cash flows early and are a prominent example of reinvestment rate risk.","A long-term coupon Treasury bond":"Long-term coupon bonds are highly exposed to reinvestment rate risk.","A bond fund holding 20-year coupon bonds":"Coupon income must be reinvested, so the risk remains."},"Theo, a U.S. investor, owns a Japanese bond that pays &yen;10,000 of interest. When he bought it, $1 bought &yen;100; today $1 buys &yen;125. What happened to the dollar value of each interest payment?":{"The dollar weakened; each payment is now worth $125, up $25.":"More yen per dollar means the dollar strengthened, and the payment converts to fewer dollars.","The dollar strengthened; each payment is now worth $125, up $25.":"Multiplying yen by the rate instead of dividing gives the wrong direction.","The dollar weakened; each payment is now worth $80, down $20.":"The amount is right, but a dollar that buys more yen has strengthened."},"Joanne keeps 80% of her long-term savings in CDs and money market funds \"to be safe.\" Which risk is she most exposed to?":{"Business risk":"CDs and money market funds have little firm-specific operating exposure.","Exchange rate risk":"Her holdings are domestic, so currency moves are not the main concern.","Marketability risk":"Cash equivalents are easy to convert; they help manage liquidity."},"The Harbor Growth Fund has a standard deviation of 24, the market index has a standard deviation of 15, and their correlation coefficient is 0.70. What is the fund's beta?":{"1.60":"This is 24 &divide; 15 without multiplying by the correlation.","0.44":"This inverts the ratio: 0.70 &times; 15 &divide; 24.","0.07":"This divides by the market variance (225) instead of the market standard deviation."},"A stock's covariance with the market is 180, and the market's standard deviation is 15. What is the stock's beta?":{"12.00":"This divides covariance by the standard deviation instead of the variance.","1.25":"This inverts the formula: variance &divide; covariance.","0.64":"This squares the beta, confusing it with R-squared."},"Ana's $100,000 portfolio holds $50,000 of a stock with a beta of 1.40, $30,000 of a stock with a beta of 0.90, and $20,000 of a stock with a beta of 0.50. What is the portfolio's weighted beta?":{"0.93":"This is the simple average of the three betas, ignoring dollar weights.","1.40":"This uses only the largest holding's beta.","2.80":"This adds the betas instead of weighting them."},"Beta is the most appropriate measure of risk for which of the following?":{"A single, undiversified stock position":"A lone stock carries unsystematic risk; standard deviation captures total risk.","Any asset, whether or not it is diversified":"That describes standard deviation, which works for all assets.","An asset with a very low correlation to the market":"Low correlation makes beta unreliable."},"A gold-mining fund has a standard deviation of 30 versus 15 for the S&amp;P 500, yet its beta against the S&amp;P 500 is only 0.40. Which conclusion is best?":{"The fund is 60% less volatile than the overall market.":"Its standard deviation is twice the market's, so it is not less volatile.","The fund has less total risk than an S&amp;P 500 index fund.":"Total risk is measured by standard deviation, which is higher here.","The fund's standard deviation figure must be misreported.":"Nothing is inconsistent: beta = 2 &times; correlation, so &rho; = 0.20."},"Nadia has $60,000 in a large-cap fund that returned 11%, $25,000 in a bond fund that returned 6%, and $15,000 in a REIT that returned 4%. What was her portfolio's weighted average return?":{"7.00%":"This is the simple average of the three returns, ignoring dollar weights.","11.00%":"This uses only the largest holding's return.","21.00%":"This adds the returns instead of weighting them."},"A fund earned 12%, &minus;4%, 9%, 15%, and 3% over the last five years. Using the textbook's convention for historical returns, what is its standard deviation?":{"6.78%":"This is the population standard deviation (dividing by n = 5).","7.00%":"This is the mean return, not the standard deviation.","57.50%":"This is the sample variance; you must take the square root."},"A portfolio has a mean annual return of 8% and a standard deviation of 6%. Assuming a normal distribution, within what range should returns fall about 95% of the time?":{"2% to 14%":"That is &plusmn;1 standard deviation, which covers about 68%.","&minus;10% to 26%":"That is &plusmn;3 standard deviations, which covers about 99%.","5% to 11%":"That shifts the mean by half a standard deviation, not two."},"A stock fund has a mean return of 9% and a standard deviation of 5%. Assuming a normal distribution, about what share of annual returns would exceed 19%?":{"5%":"That is the share outside &plusmn;2 SD on both sides combined, not just the upper tail.","16%":"That is the upper tail beyond +1 SD (14%).","0.5%":"That is the upper tail beyond +3 SD (24%)."},"A fund's mean return is 10% with a standard deviation of 4%. Last year it returned 2%. What is that year's z-statistic?":{"+2.0":"The return is below the mean, so the z-score is negative.","&minus;0.5":"This divides the standard deviation by the deviation instead of the reverse.","&minus;8.0":"This is the raw deviation; it must be divided by the standard deviation."},"When projecting the ending value of an unleveraged portfolio, which distribution does the textbook say to assume?":{"Normal":"Normal is assumed for the range of annual returns, which can be negative.","Platykurtic":"Platykurtic describes peakedness, not the floor at zero.","Negatively skewed":"Ending values have a long right tail, not a long left tail."},"Which statement about return distributions is correct according to the textbook?":{"Kurtosis measures how asymmetric a distribution is around its mean.":"Asymmetry is skewness; kurtosis measures peakedness.","In a positively skewed distribution, the median lies above the mean.":"In positive skew the median is below the mean.","Semivariance measures deviations both above and below the mean.":"Semivariance counts only deviations below the mean."},"An analyst is measuring risk from five years of a fund's historical returns. Which method does the textbook expect on the CFP exam?":{"Population standard deviation, dividing by n":"Measuring a full population is rarely realistic; the exam likely asks only for the sample version.","Variance, dividing by n &minus; 1 with no square root":"Variance is the squared figure; risk is reported as standard deviation.","Semivariance, using only below-mean returns":"Semivariance is a downside measure, not the standard total-risk measure."},"A fund has an average annual return of 12%, a beta of 1.10, and a standard deviation of 18%. What is its coefficient of variation?":{"0.67":"This inverts the formula: return &divide; standard deviation.","0.09":"This divides beta by return; CV uses standard deviation, not beta.","16.36":"This divides standard deviation by beta."},"Based on the data above, which fund offers the least total risk per unit of return?":{"Fund P":"P's CV is 12 &divide; 9 = 1.33, higher than Q's 1.14.","Fund R":"R has the lowest standard deviation but the highest CV (9 &divide; 6 = 1.50).","Fund S":"S's CV is 15 &divide; 11 = 1.36, higher than Q's 1.14."},"A lower coefficient of variation indicates which of the following?":{"Less systematic risk per unit of return":"CV uses standard deviation, which measures total risk, not beta.","A lower correlation with the market":"Correlation is a separate measure of co-movement.","A higher expected return":"CV can fall from lower risk alone, with no change in return."},"Lena is choosing between a fund with a 7% mean return and 9% standard deviation and one with a 12% mean return and 14% standard deviation. Which tool best compares their risk per unit of return?":{"Covariance":"Covariance measures how two assets move together, not risk per unit of return.","Beta":"Beta captures only systematic risk relative to the market.","Coefficient of determination":"R-squared shows how much movement the benchmark explains."},"Fund A has a standard deviation of 14, Fund B has a standard deviation of 20, and their correlation coefficient is 0.45. What is their covariance?":{"15.30":"This multiplies the correlation by the sum of the standard deviations.","280.00":"This omits the correlation coefficient.","622.22":"This divides by the correlation instead of multiplying."},"Two funds have standard deviations of 12 and 16, and their covariance is 96. What is their correlation coefficient?":{"0.25":"This squares the correlation, which gives R-squared.","8.00":"This divides covariance by only one standard deviation.","0.67":"This divides by the first fund's variance (144) instead of the product of the SDs."},"A portfolio is 70% in a bond fund (standard deviation 12) and 30% in a stock fund (standard deviation 25). Their correlation is 0.20. What is the portfolio's standard deviation?":{"15.90%":"This is the weighted average SD, valid only at a correlation of +1.","11.26%":"This drops the covariance term from the formula.","152.01%":"This is the portfolio variance; take the square root."},"Under which condition does a two-asset portfolio's standard deviation equal the weighted average of the two assets' standard deviations?":{"The correlation coefficient is zero":"At zero correlation, portfolio SD is below the weighted average.","The correlation coefficient is &minus;1.0":"At &minus;1, risk is reduced most and can even reach zero.","The two assets are held in equal weights":"Weights do not matter; only a correlation of +1 allows the shortcut."},"Marcus already owns an S&amp;P 500 index fund. Which additional fund would add the most diversification, based on each fund's correlation with the S&amp;P 500?":{"Fund with a correlation of +0.93":"This fund moves almost in lockstep with what he already owns.","Fund with a correlation of +0.85":"This correlation is still high; little diversification is added.","Fund with a correlation of +0.70":"Lower than the others but still well above +0.36."},"An investor puts 50% in each of two assets that both have a standard deviation of 15 and a correlation of &minus;1.0. What is the portfolio's standard deviation?":{"15":"That is the weighted average, which applies only at a correlation of +1.","7.5":"This halves one asset's SD and ignores the covariance term.","10.6":"This uses a zero correlation: &radic;(56.25 + 56.25) = 10.6."},"A fund's correlation coefficient with its benchmark is 0.80. What percentage of the fund's movement comes from unsystematic (non-benchmark) factors?":{"20%":"This subtracts the correlation from 1 instead of R-squared.","64%":"This is R-squared, the share explained by the benchmark (systematic).","80%":"This is the correlation coefficient itself."},"A fund has a standard deviation of 20 and a covariance of 224 with the market, whose standard deviation is 16. What is the fund's R-squared, and is beta a reliable risk measure for it?":{"0.70; yes, it meets the 0.70 threshold":"0.70 is the correlation; R-squared is 0.70 squared.","0.875; yes, beta is high enough to rely on":"0.875 is the beta (224 &divide; 256), not R-squared.","0.49; yes, any positive R-squared validates beta":"Beta needs an R-squared of about 0.70 or more."},"A portfolio's R-squared with the S&amp;P 500 is 0.45. Which performance measure is appropriate?":{"Treynor ratio":"Treynor uses beta, which needs an R-squared of about 0.70 or more.","Jensen's alpha":"Jensen uses beta, which needs an R-squared of about 0.70 or more.","Treynor and Jensen together":"Both rely on beta, which is unreliable at this R-squared."},"An actively managed large-cap fund with materially higher expenses than an index fund shows an R-squared of 0.98 against the S&amp;P 500. What is the best interpretation?":{"Its manager's stock picking explains 98% of its returns.":"R-squared is the share explained by the benchmark, not by the manager.","It is an excellent diversifier for an S&amp;P 500 index fund.":"An R-squared that high means it moves with the index and diversifies little.","Its beta is unreliable because R-squared is too high.":"A high R-squared makes beta more reliable, not less."},"Dana's investment produced a total return of 45% over six years. What is her simple (noncompounded) annual rate of return?":{"6.39%":"This is the compound annual rate, 1.45<sup>1/6</sup> &minus; 1, not the simple rate the question asks for.","9.00%":"This divides by 5 instead of the six years Dana held the investment.","45.00%":"This is the cumulative return for the whole period, not an annual rate."},"A fund returned 10%, &minus;5%, 18% and 7% in four consecutive years. Which pair correctly states its arithmetic mean and geometric mean?":{"Arithmetic 7.18%; geometric 7.50%":"The two values are swapped; the geometric mean can never exceed the arithmetic mean.","Arithmetic 10.00%; geometric 7.18%":"This treats the &minus;5% year as a +5% gain when averaging.","Arithmetic 7.50%; geometric 7.98%":"7.98% is the cumulative 31.94% divided by 4, which is not a geometric mean."},"Which statement about the arithmetic and geometric mean of a return series is CORRECT?":{"The geometric mean exceeds the arithmetic mean when returns are volatile.":"Volatility pushes the geometric mean further below the arithmetic mean, not above it.","The arithmetic mean assumes each period's return is reinvested.":"The arithmetic mean is a simple average and does not assume reinvestment.","The geometric mean ignores the effect of compounding.":"Compounding is exactly what the geometric mean captures; the arithmetic mean ignores it."},"Based on these facts, what is the time-weighted return on the stock?":{"15.37%":"15.37% is the dollar-weighted return, which includes Omar's second purchase.","14.02%":"This leaves out the $2 dividend: (52 &divide; 40)<sup>1/2</sup> &minus; 1.","30.00%":"This is the two-year price gain on one share, not an annualized total return."},"Based on these facts, what is Omar's dollar-weighted return?":{"16.54%":"16.54% is the time-weighted return, which ignores Omar's added purchase.","13.69%":"This drops the $2 dividend from the Year 1 cash flow (CF1 = &minus;46).","63.76%":"This counts the ending value of two shares but never subtracts the $46 purchase."},"Ben's account statement shows a lower return than the mutual fund's published return for the same period, even though he owned only that fund. What is the best explanation?":{"Published fund returns are dollar-weighted across all shareholders.":"Funds generally report time-weighted, not dollar-weighted, returns.","Time-weighted returns include investor contributions, so they always run higher.":"It is the dollar-weighted return, not the time-weighted one, that includes investor cash flows.","The fund is quoting its SEC yield rather than any total return measure.":"Nothing suggests a yield was quoted; the gap comes from cash-flow timing."},"Using the textbook's convention for annualizing a periodic IRR, what is Rosa's dollar-weighted return?":{"2.40%":"2.40% is the quarterly IRR before annualizing.","9.75%":"This is a simple HPR, ($300 gain + $480 dividends) &divide; $8,000, which ignores timing.","9.97%":"This compounds the quarterly rate, (1.024044)<sup>4</sup> &minus; 1; the textbook multiplies by 4."},"A corporate bond has a 7% nominal return and inflation is 2.5%. Using the formula expected on the CFP exam, what is the real rate of return?":{"4.50%":"Nominal minus inflation is a shortcut, not the exact real-return formula.","9.68%":"This multiplies (1.07 &times; 1.025) instead of dividing.","6.83%":"This divides 7% by 1.025 without converting the nominal rate to 1 + r."},"Priya holds a 7% corporate bond, has a 30% combined federal and state marginal tax rate, and inflation is 2.5%. What is her after-tax, inflation-adjusted return?":{"2.40%":"This subtracts inflation from the after-tax rate instead of using the real-return formula.","4.39%":"This is the real return before taxes.","3.07%":"This applies the tax to the pre-tax real return; tax the nominal rate first, then adjust for inflation."},"A credit card charges 1.5% interest per month, compounded monthly. What are the card's APR and effective annual rate?":{"APR 19.56%; EAR 18.00%":"The labels are reversed; the APR is the uncompounded figure.","APR 1.50%; EAR 19.56%":"1.5% is the monthly periodic rate, not the annual percentage rate.","APR 18.00%; EAR 18.00%":"The EAR equals the APR only with annual compounding; monthly compounding raises it."},"Luis bought a bond for $960, collected $140 of interest, and sold it for $1,010. What is his holding period return?":{"5.21%":"This counts only the price gain and leaves out the interest.","14.58%":"This counts only the interest and leaves out the price gain.","18.81%":"This divides by the ending value; HPR uses the beginning value."},"Kendra earned an HPR of 20% on an investment she held for three years. Which statement is CORRECT?":{"The HPR understates the investment's true annual return.":"Understatement happens when the holding period is shorter than one year.","The HPR equals the investment's compound annual return.":"A three-year HPR is cumulative; the annual compound rate would be far lower.","The HPR already reflects when each cash flow occurred.":"Ignoring cash-flow timing is the HPR's major weakness."},"Based on these facts, what is the portfolio's Jensen's alpha?":{"+2.00%":"This compares the portfolio with the market return and ignores beta.","&minus;0.80%":"The sign is reversed; actual minus required is positive here.","+8.00%":"This is the excess return over the risk-free rate, not over the CAPM return."},"Based on these facts, what is the portfolio's Treynor ratio (in percentage points)?":{"9.17":"This divides the total return by beta without subtracting the risk-free rate.","1.67":"This subtracts the market return instead of the risk-free rate.","0.50":"0.50 is the Sharpe ratio, which divides by standard deviation."},"Based on these facts, what is the portfolio's Sharpe ratio?":{"0.69":"This divides the total return by standard deviation without subtracting the risk-free rate.","6.67":"6.67 is the Treynor ratio, which divides by beta.","0.13":"This subtracts the market return instead of the risk-free rate."},"Which fund showed better risk-adjusted performance, and why?":{"Fund L, because it has the higher Treynor ratio":"Treynor relies on beta, which is unreliable with R&sup2; this low.","Fund L, because it has the higher Sharpe ratio":"Fund L's Sharpe ratio is 0.50, below Fund K's 0.58.","Fund K, because it has the higher Treynor ratio":"Fund K's Treynor ratio (7.78) is lower than L's (9.09), and Treynor should not be used here anyway."},"A portfolio's Jensen's alpha is negative. Which statement must be true?":{"It earned less than the risk-free rate.":"It can beat the risk-free rate and still fall short of its CAPM required return.","It has a beta below 1.0.":"Alpha's sign says nothing about the size of beta.","Its Sharpe ratio is below the market's.":"Sharpe uses total risk; a negative alpha does not settle how Sharpe ratios compare."},"Fund Q returned 9.5% with a tracking error of 3%. Fund R returned 10% with a tracking error of 6%. Their benchmark returned 8%. Which fund has the better information ratio?":{"Fund R, with an IR of 0.33":"0.33 is Fund R's IR, but it is lower than Fund Q's.","Fund R, with an IR of 1.67":"This divides Fund R's total return by tracking error without subtracting the benchmark.","Fund Q, with an IR of 3.17":"This divides Fund Q's total return by tracking error without subtracting the benchmark."},"The information ratio becomes identical to the Sharpe ratio when":{"the portfolio's beta equals 1.0":"A beta of 1.0 does not turn active risk into total risk.","the tracking error equals zero":"Zero tracking error would make the ratio undefined.","the benchmark is the S&amp;P 500 Index":"An equity index benchmark keeps the measure an active-return ratio."},"Which performance measure is an ABSOLUTE measure rather than a relative one?":{"Sharpe ratio":"The Sharpe ratio is relative; it must be compared with another portfolio or the market.","Treynor ratio":"The Treynor ratio is relative; it must be compared with another portfolio or the market.","Information ratio":"The information ratio is a ratio of active return to active risk, judged against other managers."},"In a price-weighted index such as the DJIA, how does a $90 stock's influence compare with a $30 stock's, regardless of company size?":{"One-third as much":"This reverses the relationship; the higher-priced stock has more influence.","About the same":"Price weighting gives unequal influence based on share price.","It depends on each firm's market capitalization":"Market capitalization drives weights in cap-weighted indices, not price-weighted ones."},"A company with a $45 billion market value belongs to a capitalization-weighted index whose members total $300 billion. What share of the index's price movement does it account for?":{"0.20%":"This assumes equal weighting across 500 stocks rather than cap weighting.","6.67%":"This inverts the ratio ($300B &divide; $45B), giving a multiple rather than a weight.","85.00%":"This is the share held by all the other companies, not this one."},"Which of the following is NOT a shortcoming of the Dow Jones Industrial Average?":{"It ignores cash dividends.":"Failing to reflect cash dividends is a recognized DJIA weakness.","It gives higher-priced stocks more influence.":"Price weighting, which favors high-priced shares, is a recognized weakness.","It relies on a small sample of mostly blue-chip stocks.":"Its 30-stock, blue-chip sample is a recognized source of bias."},"Marta manages a fund of developed-market stocks from Europe, Australasia and the Far East, excluding the U.S. and Canada. Which benchmark fits best?":{"Russell 2000 Index":"The Russell 2000 measures U.S. small-cap stocks.","Wilshire 5000 Index":"The Wilshire 5000 measures the broad U.S. market.","S&amp;P 500 Index":"The S&amp;P 500 measures U.S. large-cap stocks."},"Which conclusion about the client's 7.9% return is most appropriate?":{"It lagged the S&amp;P 500 by 2.1%, so the portfolio underperformed.":"Measuring the whole portfolio against the S&amp;P 500 ignores the 50% held in other asset classes.","It beat a blended benchmark of 6.67%.":"6.67% is a simple average of the three index returns, which ignores the client's weights.","It beat the Russell 2000 by 1.9%, so the manager added value.":"A single small-cap index ignores the 70% of the portfolio held in other asset classes."},"A bond with a 4.5% coupon is trading at $920. What is its current yield?":{"4.50%":"4.5% is the coupon (nominal) yield, based on par rather than price.","4.14%":"This multiplies the coupon rate by the price ratio instead of dividing interest by price.","2.45%":"This uses the semiannual payment ($22.50) instead of the annual interest."},"An 8-year bond with a 5% coupon paid semiannually is priced at $940. What is its yield to maturity?":{"2.98%":"2.98% is the semiannual rate; it must be doubled (or use 2 P/Yr).","5.32%":"5.32% is the current yield ($50 &divide; $940), which ignores the gain to par.","5.00%":"5% is the coupon rate; a discount bond's YTM is above its coupon."},"Based on these facts, what is the bond's yield to call?":{"5.93%":"5.93% is the yield to maturity (FV 1,000, N 20).","6.48%":"6.48% is the current yield ($70 &divide; $1,080).","4.78%":"This uses par ($1,000) instead of the $1,020 call price."},"A 15-year bond with a 6% coupon trades at $930 and is callable at par in 5 years. Which ordering of its yields is expected?":{"Coupon &gt; current yield &gt; YTM &gt; YTC":"This is the premium-bond ordering, reversed.","YTM &gt; YTC &gt; current yield &gt; coupon":"Reaching par sooner (at the call) raises the YTC above the YTM for a discount bond.","Current yield &gt; YTC &gt; YTM &gt; coupon":"Current yield ignores the gain to par, so it falls below both YTM and YTC."},"A 10-year zero-coupon bond is priced at $600. Using the textbook's default conventions, what is its yield to maturity?":{"5.24%":"This uses annual compounding (N = 10), but the default is semiannual even for zeros.","2.59%":"2.59% is the semiannual rate before annualizing.","6.67%":"This is a simple average gain ($400 &divide; $600 &divide; 10 years) that ignores compounding."},"Which assumption is built into a bond's yield to maturity?":{"Coupons are spent as received rather than reinvested.":"The IRR math assumes reinvestment, not spending.","Coupons are reinvested at the bond's coupon rate.":"The reinvestment rate assumed is the YTM, not the coupon rate.","The bond is redeemed at its first call date.":"Redemption at the first call date is the yield-to-call assumption."},"A municipal bond yields 3.6%. The investor's marginal federal rate is 35% and her state has no income tax. What is the bond's taxable equivalent yield?":{"2.34%":"This multiplies by (1 &minus; t), which converts a taxable yield to after-tax, not the reverse.","4.86%":"This multiplies by (1 + t) instead of dividing by (1 &minus; t).","10.29%":"This divides by the tax rate instead of by (1 &minus; tax rate)."},"Using the textbook's method for a double tax-exempt bond, what is its taxable equivalent yield?":{"4.86%":"Simply adding the two rates (3.4% &divide; 0.70) is the non-itemizer shortcut.","4.47%":"This ignores the state tax exemption (3.4% &divide; 0.76).","2.43%":"This multiplies by the two factors instead of dividing."},"Felix can buy a 6% corporate bond or a 4.3% municipal bond of similar quality and maturity. His marginal federal rate is 32% and his state has no income tax. Based on yield alone, which should he prefer?":{"The corporate, because 6% beats the muni's stated 4.3%":"Comparing a pre-tax yield with a tax-free yield ignores taxes.","The corporate, because the muni's TEY is only 2.92%":"This multiplies the muni yield by (1 &minus; t) instead of dividing.","The municipal, because its TEY of 13.44% exceeds 6%":"This divides by the tax rate rather than by (1 &minus; tax rate)."},"Which of the following is one of Markowitz's assumptions underlying modern portfolio theory?":{"Investors can borrow and lend unlimited sums at the risk-free rate":"Unlimited borrowing/lending at the risk-free rate is a CAPM assumption added later by Sharpe, not part of Markowitz's list.","Investors measure portfolio risk with beta instead of standard deviation":"MPT measures risk with standard deviation; beta is the CAPM/SML risk measure.","Investor indifference strengthens the further they look past the holding period":"MPT assumes indifference diminishes beyond the holding period, not that it strengthens."},"Based on these facts, which conclusion is correct?":{"The current portfolio is efficient because it can actually be built today":"Being attainable is not the same as efficient; the current portfolio earns less than the frontier offers at 12% risk.","The proposed portfolio dominates and should be implemented right away":"A 10% return at 12% risk lies above the frontier, so it cannot be constructed from this asset universe.","Both portfolios are efficient because they carry the same risk level":"Equal risk does not make portfolios efficient; only the frontier point (8%) is efficient at that risk."},"Compared with a moderately risk-tolerant investor, a highly risk-averse investor's indifference curves are":{"flatter, touching the efficient frontier at a lower-risk point":"Flat curves belong to risk-tolerant investors, not risk-averse ones.","steeper, touching the efficient frontier at a higher-risk point":"Steep curves produce a tangency to the left (lower risk), not to the right.","downward sloping, since added risk lowers the return demanded":"Indifference curves slope upward because more risk requires more return."},"Investor A's indifference curve is tangent to the efficient frontier at a portfolio with a 10% standard deviation; Investor B's is tangent at 18%. Which statement is most accurate?":{"B has steeper indifference curves and is more risk averse than A":"A steeper curve would place the tangency to the left (lower risk), the opposite of B's position.","A's portfolio is inefficient because it has a lower expected return":"Both tangency points lie on the frontier, so both portfolios are efficient.","No conclusion about risk attitudes can be drawn from tangency points":"The tangency location reveals the slope of each investor's curves and therefore relative risk tolerance."},"Dana owns 14 mutual funds. Nine are large-cap U.S. stock funds whose top-10 holdings largely repeat. Under MPT principles, what is the best observation?":{"More funds always reduce unsystematic risk, so she should add more":"Funds holding many securities add no further unsystematic-risk reduction and may dilute performance.","She holds too few securities to eliminate unsystematic risk":"Through 14 funds she indirectly owns thousands of issues; far fewer are needed to diversify.","Overlap lowers her correlation with bonds, which reduces total risk":"Overlap concentrates the same stocks; it does nothing to lower correlation with bonds."},"A portfolio has risen in value since it was built, and the advisor has new cash to invest. Under the textbook's correlation guidance, which addition would most likely lower portfolio standard deviation?":{"The portfolio's best performer, to add to existing momentum":"Adding more of a top performer raises concentration and correlation, not diversification.","A fund highly correlated with current holdings, for consistency":"High correlation with existing holdings gives little or no risk reduction.","Whichever available security has the highest standard deviation":"A high standalone standard deviation says nothing about how the asset interacts with the portfolio; correlation drives the benefit."},"A stock has a beta of 1.4. The T-bill yields 3.5%, and the market is expected to return 9.5%. What is the stock's required return under CAPM?":{"13.30%":"13.30% multiplies the market return by beta and ignores the risk-free rate entirely.","8.40%":"8.40% is only the stock risk premium; the risk-free rate must be added back.","16.80%":"16.80% applies beta to the full market return instead of to the market risk premium."},"The market is expected to return 8.5% and T-bills yield 2.5%. For a stock with a beta of 0.7, what are the market risk premium and the stock risk premium?":{"Market risk premium 8.5%; stock risk premium 5.95%":"The market risk premium subtracts the risk-free rate: 8.5% &minus; 2.5% = 6.0%, not 8.5%.","Market risk premium 6.0%; stock risk premium 6.7%":"6.7% is the full CAPM required return (2.5% + 4.2%), not the stock risk premium.","Market risk premium 4.2%; stock risk premium 6.0%":"The two figures are reversed; the market premium is 6.0% and the stock premium is 6.0% &times; 0.7."},"Based on CAPM, what is the best recommendation for Priya?":{"Buy: CAPM expects 14.25%, above her 12% required return":"14.25% applies beta to the whole 9% market return rather than to the 6% premium.","Pass: CAPM expects 7.5%, below her 12% required return":"7.5% is only the risk premium (6% &times; 1.25); the 3% risk-free rate was left out.","Buy: a beta above 1.0 ensures it will beat her 12% target":"A beta above 1.0 raises the expected return, but CAPM still gives only 10.5% here."},"An asset has a beta of &minus;0.4. The risk-free rate is 4% and the market is expected to return 11%. What is its CAPM required return?":{"6.80%":"6.80% ignores the negative sign on beta and adds the premium instead of subtracting it.","&minus;2.80%":"&minus;2.80% is only the (negative) stock risk premium; the 4% risk-free rate must be added.","&minus;0.40%":"&minus;0.40% multiplies beta by the market return (11%) instead of the market risk premium (7%)."},"Using CAPM, which conclusion about Stocks Q and R is correct?":{"Q plots below the SML (sell); R plots above the SML (buy)":"This reverses the result: Q's forecast beats its 9.4% requirement, while R's falls short of 13.6%.","Both plot on the SML because both forecasts exceed the risk-free rate":"Plotting on the SML requires expected return to equal the CAPM required return, not merely exceed the risk-free rate.","R is the better buy because its forecast return is the higher one":"A higher raw forecast ignores risk; R's forecast is below what its beta of 1.6 requires."},"The risk-free rate is 3%. The market portfolio is expected to return 10% with a 16% standard deviation. Using the CML, what is the expected return of an efficient portfolio with a 12% standard deviation?":{"7.50%":"7.50% scales the market return by 12/16 but omits the risk-free rate and the premium structure.","12.33%":"12.33% inverts the ratio, multiplying the premium by 16/12 instead of 12/16.","10.50%":"10.50% uses the full 10% market return instead of the 7% market risk premium in the slope."},"Expected inflation rises and the risk-free rate moves from 3.0% to 4.5%; the market risk premium stays at 6%. What happens to the required return on a stock with a beta of 1.2?":{"It rises from 10.2% to 12.0% as the SML becomes steeper":"The slope (market risk premium) is unchanged, so beta does not multiply the 1.5% increase.","It stays at 10.2% because inflation changes only the slope":"Higher inflation raises the risk-free intercept, which lifts every asset's required return.","It falls to 8.7% because higher rates cut required returns":"A higher risk-free rate raises, not lowers, required returns."},"Investors in general become more risk averse, while the risk-free rate is unchanged. What happens to the SML?":{"It shifts upward, parallel to the original line":"A parallel shift comes from a change in the risk-free rate (e.g., inflation), not in risk aversion.","It pivots at the risk-free rate and becomes flatter":"A flatter SML reflects investors becoming less concerned about risk.","Its intercept falls while its slope is unchanged":"Greater risk aversion changes the price of risk (slope), not the risk-free intercept."},"Using APT, what is the expected return on this stock?":{"9.30%":"9.30% sums the factor terms but leaves out the 2.5% risk-free return (a<sub>0</sub>).","14.50%":"14.50% adds the raw risk premiums without weighting them by the sensitivities.","13.30%":"13.30% ignores the 0.5 sensitivity and adds the full 3% interest-rate premium."},"Which of the following is NOT one of the four primary APT factors identified in the textbook?":{"Inflation":"Inflation is one of the four primary APT factors.","Industrial production (changes in GDP)":"Industrial production, or changes in GDP, is one of the four primary factors.","The yield curve (interest rates)":"The yield curve (interest rates) is one of the four primary factors."},"Which of the following is one of the assumptions Sharpe stipulated for CAPM?":{"Investors hold differing expectations about each asset's return distribution":"CAPM assumes homogeneous expectations: all investors estimate identical distributions.","Taxes and transaction costs are central to every investment decision":"CAPM assumes there are no taxes or transaction costs.","Investors can borrow only at rates well above the risk-free rate":"CAPM assumes investors can borrow and lend at the risk-free rate."},"Under the semistrong form of the efficient market hypothesis, which approach could still produce above-market returns?":{"Fundamental analysis of financial statements":"Semistrong efficiency says public financial statement data is already in the price.","Charting support and resistance levels":"Technical analysis has no value under any form of the EMH.","Buying right after favorable earnings news":"Public announcements are priced in quickly, so buying afterward gives no edge."},"Which statements are correct?":{"I and II only":"Statement II is wrong: no form of the EMH supports technical analysis.","II and III only":"Statement II is wrong, and statements I and IV are also correct.","III only":"Statements I and IV are also correct."},"Which type of research has tended to SUPPORT the semistrong form of the EMH?":{"Return-prediction studies of January and weekend effects":"Return-prediction studies uncovered anomalies that challenge semistrong efficiency.","Cross-sectional studies showing low-P/E stocks outperform":"Cross-sectional anomalies such as the low P/E effect are evidence against semistrong efficiency.","Studies showing insiders earn abnormal trading profits":"Insider profits are evidence against the strong form, not support for the semistrong form."},"Jonah buys small-company stocks each December and sells them in January. Which anomaly is he using, and what caveat applies?":{"Weekend effect; price moves may not cover transaction costs":"The weekend effect involves buying late Monday and selling late Friday, not a December-January cycle.","Neglected-firm effect; it may just be an extension of the size effect":"The neglected-firm effect concerns stocks with little analyst coverage, not calendar timing.","Dividend-yield anomaly; higher-yielding stocks tend to outperform":"The dividend-yield anomaly is about payout levels, not buying in December."},"In a semistrong-form test, CAPM sets a stock's required return at 9%. Over the period, the stock actually returns 12.5%. What abnormal return do researchers attribute to it?":{"12.5%":"12.5% is the total return; abnormal return is only the portion above the CAPM requirement.","&minus;3.5%":"The sign is reversed; the stock beat its required return, so the abnormal return is positive.","21.5%":"21.5% adds the required and actual returns instead of taking the difference."},"A client believes U.S. large-cap stocks are highly efficient but emerging markets are not. Which implementation best fits that view?":{"Use an active manager for large caps and index emerging markets":"This reverses the logic: active management fits the less efficient segment.","Use active managers everywhere, since some segments are inefficient":"Paying for active management in efficient segments is unlikely to add value.","Avoid emerging markets entirely because their statements are unreliable":"Information gaps are exactly what create opportunities for skilled active managers."},"According to the textbook, why have low-P/E and high book-to-market stocks tended to deliver higher returns?":{"They are fundamentally strong firms with highly predictable earnings growth":"These stocks usually show poor ratios versus industry peers; \"good\" predictable firms get low required returns.","They carry lower betas, and CAPM rewards low beta with higher returns":"CAPM pays more for higher beta, not lower; that would not explain the outperformance.","Securities laws restrict insiders from trading them, reducing competition":"The textbook ties the effect to investor neglect and perceived risk, not insider trading rules."},"According to the Brinson, Hood, and Beebower study, what explains more than 90% of the variability of a portfolio's returns?":{"Security selection":"Security selection explained only a small share of return variability.","Market timing":"Market timing explained only a small share of return variability.","Expense ratios":"The study attributed the variability to asset allocation, not to costs."},"A prospect wants to hire only managers who consistently rank in the top 25% of their asset class. What does the textbook suggest?":{"Most active funds beat their indexes, so this is easy to accomplish":"Well over half of mutual funds fail to outperform their indexes.","Top-quartile consistency is common among large-cap growth funds":"The textbook calls consistent top-quartile performance rare, without a large-cap exception.","A past top-quartile ranking virtually ensures future outperformance":"Regression to the mean is common, so past ranking does not ensure future results."},"A client wants to keep $60,000 available for a home down payment in three years. In which IPS content area should this be recorded?":{"Return requirement":"The return requirement states the target return, not near-term cash needs.","Laws and regulations":"Laws and regulations cover items like ERISA compliance for plans, rarely individuals.","Permitted and excluded investments":"This area lists asset types the client wants included or excluded."},"How should Walter's IPS describe his risk tolerance?":{"Low ability and low willingness to take risk":"His wealth, health, insurance and pension give him a high ability to bear risk.","High ability and high willingness, since wealth sets tolerance":"Wealth drives ability, but his distress at any decline shows low willingness.","Low ability but high willingness to take risk":"This reverses both dimensions of his profile."},"An IPS targets a 4.5% real return, and the advisor assumes 2.5% average inflation. Using the textbook's simple approach, what nominal return requirement should the IPS state?":{"2.0%":"2.0% subtracts inflation instead of adding it.","4.5%":"4.5% ignores inflation, so the portfolio would lose purchasing-power ground.","11.25%":"11.25% multiplies the two rates instead of adding them."},"Research shows that the pain of a loss outweighs the pleasure of an equal gain. Which concept, introduced by Kahneman and Tversky, does this describe?":{"Risk aversion":"Risk aversion is preferring a sure gain over a gamble with the same expected value.","Overconfidence":"Overconfidence is overrating one's ability to predict or beat the market.","Representativeness":"Representativeness is assuming recent patterns will continue."},"An advisor runs a Monte Carlo retirement projection using the returns of the Wilshire 5000, but the client's funds carry meaningful fees. What is the main risk?":{"It will understate success because it ignores the market's positive bias":"Simulating a fee-free index ignores costs, which biases results upward, not downward.","Monte Carlo cannot model withdrawals, so the result is meaningless":"Monte Carlo can model additions and withdrawals at each period; that is a strength.","Fees matter only in taxable accounts, so there is no distortion":"Fees reduce returns in every account type."},"A client expects the Fed to keep raising rates over the next year and wants to stay flexible. Which holding best fits the textbook's guidance?":{"A long-term investment-grade corporate bond fund":"Long-term bonds lose value as rates rise and lock in today's lower yield.","Shares of a highly leveraged electric utility":"Utilities often trade on yield and carry heavy debt, so rising rates hurt them.","A 20-year zero-coupon Treasury bond":"Long zero-coupon bonds are among the most rate-sensitive holdings."},"As an economy begins recovering from a recession, which pattern does the textbook describe?":{"Consumer noncyclicals lead as investors seek steady, reliable demand":"Noncyclicals are favored when rates rise and a slowdown looms, not early recovery.","Basic materials and energy lead on renewed inflation fears":"Materials and energy tend to lead later in the cycle when inflation fears return.","Utilities rally because interest rates are climbing quickly":"Rising rates hurt utilities, which trade on yield and carry heavy debt."},"Inflation is accelerating sharply. Which holding is most likely to hold up, according to the textbook?":{"A long-term Treasury bond":"Rising inflation almost always lifts rates and pushes bond prices down, especially long bonds.","An intermediate-term corporate bond fund":"Bond funds lose value as inflation drives interest rates higher.","A heavily indebted bank stock":"Financial firms and high-debt companies are especially hurt by rising rates."},"Which statement best describes sensitivity analysis?":{"Running thousands of random trials to build a probability distribution":"Thousands of random trials describes Monte Carlo simulation (stochastic modeling).","Measuring a stock's volatility relative to the overall market":"Volatility relative to the market is beta.","Shifting among industries as the business cycle progresses":"Moving among industries over the cycle is rotational investing."},"Which statement about tactical asset allocation (TAA) is correct?":{"It holds a fixed mix until a major life event calls for a change":"Holding a fixed mix until a life event describes strategic asset allocation.","It usually has lower turnover and lower costs than strategic allocation":"TAA's frequent reallocation makes transaction and turnover costs much higher.","It is generally preferred by passive, buy-and-hold investors":"Passive, buy-and-hold investors tend toward strategic allocation; active managers favor TAA."},"A lifecycle (target-date) 401(k) fund gradually shifts toward bonds as participants age, regardless of market conditions. This is an example of":{"tactical asset allocation based on market timing":"TAA shifts the mix based on market conditions, which this fund explicitly ignores.","sector rotation across the business cycle":"Sector rotation moves among industries as the business cycle changes.","a core-and-satellite investment strategy":"Core and satellite pairs passive index holdings with higher-risk active positions."},"A $500,000 portfolio has a strategic target of 60% stocks / 40% bonds. After a rally, stocks are 70% of the portfolio. How much stock must be sold to restore the target?":{"$35,000":"$35,000 is 10% of the $350,000 stock position, not the excess over target.","$100,000":"$100,000 doubles the actual drift.","$30,000":"$30,000 is 10% of the $300,000 target, not the dollar overweight."},"How much is invested in emerging-market equity in this core-and-satellite portfolio?":{"$360,000":"$360,000 applies 30% to the whole portfolio instead of to the satellite sleeve.","$288,000":"$288,000 applies 30% to the core sleeve rather than the satellite sleeve.","$60,000":"$60,000 uses the 25% REIT weight instead of the 30% emerging-market weight."},"Which strategy best fits Lorraine's goals?":{"Contribute the shares to a diversified exchange fund":"An exchange fund diversifies without immediate capital gains tax but creates no charitable deduction.","Gift the shares outright to her adult son":"A gift may trigger gift tax and gives her no charitable deduction to offset compensation.","Sell the shares to the company's ESOP":"Selling to an ESOP creates a market for the shares but generates no charitable deduction."},"A global pandemic triggers a sharp market selloff. How does the textbook classify this source of risk?":{"Endogenous":"Endogenous risks arise inside the financial system itself, such as the 2008-2009 crisis.","Unsystematic":"A pandemic hits the whole market; it is not company-specific risk.","Incremental":"Incremental describes small changes in a client's situation, not a source of crisis risk."},"Which client event most clearly calls for redesigning the investment policy statement?":{"Receiving a 4% raise at her annual performance review":"A modest raise is incremental change: worth watching, not a reason to rewrite the IPS.","A sudden 6% one-week drop in the S&amp;P 500 Index":"Short-term market swings are noise that an IPS is designed to help clients ignore.","Her bond fund trailing its benchmark slightly this quarter":"Minor benchmark lag is noise, not a change in the client's circumstances."},"During a sharp bear market, Corinne, 44, with a long time horizon and a 75/25 stock/bond IPS, demands to sell her stocks. Which advisor response best fits the textbook?":{"Move her to cash now and re-enter once volatility subsides":"Moving to cash risks trading losses and missing the early stages of the recovery.","Shift the portfolio to all Treasuries until the economy recovers":"Abandoning the IPS for short-term conditions is the micromanaging the textbook warns against.","Switch to tactical allocation so she can time the rebound":"Market timing contradicts the long-term policy that should guide her through the crisis."},"Before any crisis, which step best prepares a client to absorb a sudden job loss without disrupting the long-term portfolio?":{"Count on federal stimulus programs being offered again":"Government relief is not guaranteed in a future crisis; the textbook warns not to assume it.","Hold a large single-stock position as a flexible reserve":"A concentrated stock position adds unsystematic risk and may be down when cash is needed.","Plan to borrow on margin against the stock portfolio":"Margin borrowing adds leverage just when markets are falling."},"After vivid news coverage of a market crash, a client becomes convinced another one is imminent. Which heuristic is most likely at work?":{"Anchoring and adjustment":"Anchoring ties decisions to a reference point, such as a purchase price.","Familiarity":"Familiarity pushes investors toward what they know, at the cost of diversification.","Overconfidence":"Overconfidence makes investors overrate their skill at predicting or beating the market."},"In which section of the Calloways' investment policy statement (IPS) should these two needs be recorded?":{"Return requirement":"This section states the target return (absolute or relative, before or after tax and inflation), not cash the clients must be able to reach.","Laws and regulations":"For individuals this section is usually minor; it matters mainly for retirement plans or endowments subject to rules such as ERISA.","Permitted and excluded investments":"This section lists holdings the clients want included or ruled out (for example, for social or religious reasons), not near-term cash needs."},"Which statement best describes the Calloways' risk tolerance for the IPS?":{"Low ability and low willingness; the IPS should target capital preservation and leave the 6.5% goal as is":"Their wealth, income, insurance and 12-year horizon give them a high ability to take risk, and a goal needing 6.5% cannot simply stay unchanged under a capital-preservation mix.","High ability and high willingness; their net worth lets the IPS set above-average volatility":"Graham&rsquo;s panic selling and fear of a 15% drop show low willingness, which the IPS must address rather than ignore.","Low ability but high willingness; the planner should rely on the questionnaire score alone":"Ability is high, not low, and a questionnaire score alone ignores the behavior Graham has already shown."},"How large is the gap in the Calloways' emergency reserve, and what is the best way to close it?":{"$19,000; arrange a 401(k) loan that can be drawn if an emergency occurs":"A retirement-plan loan is borrowing, not a reserve; a liquid money market holding is the preferred way to meet an unexpected crisis.","$81,000; open a home equity line of credit to serve as the whole reserve":"$81,000 is the full target, not the gap, and a credit line is borrowing, not a liquid reserve the clients own.","$39,000; leave the CD out of the reserve and draw the gap from the 401(k)s":"A bank CD is a cash equivalent that can be redeemed (at most with an interest penalty), so excluding it overstates the gap, and 401(k) money is not a reserve."},"Which response by the planner is most accurate?":{"She is right; both accounts carry FDIC coverage up to $250,000 per ownership category":"Only the bank MMDA is FDIC insured; a money market mutual fund is an investment company product without federal insurance.","She is right only if the fund holds Treasury bills, which makes the fund itself FDIC insured":"Holding Treasury bills improves a fund&rsquo;s credit quality but does not give the fund FDIC coverage.","Neither account is insured, but only the bank account permits check-writing":"The MMDA is federally insured, and money market mutual funds also commonly allow withdrawals by check."},"What is the Calloways' after-tax, inflation-adjusted return on the T-bills?":{"0.97%":"0.97% is the pre-tax real return (1.04 &divide; 1.03 &minus; 1); it ignores federal tax on the interest.","1.00%":"1.00% just subtracts inflation from the nominal yield and ignores both tax and the proper real-return formula.","&minus;0.47%":"This applies the 5% state rate as well, but T-bill interest is exempt from state income tax."},"What is the planner's best course of action with Graham?":{"Move the 401(k) to stable value now and re-enter after the market regains half its loss":"Waiting for a partial recovery is market timing; it tends to miss the early part of a rebound and locks in the loss.","Shift half the 401(k) to stable value as a compromise to ease Graham&rsquo;s anxiety":"Cutting stocks by half still reacts to the market rather than to any change in the clients&rsquo; goals, horizon or resources.","Rewrite the IPS around capital preservation because a 25% decline changes their goals":"The decline does not change their goals, horizon, jobs or reserves; the IPS exists to keep them from reacting to this kind of turmoil."},"Which event(s) should prompt the planner to reassess the assumptions in the Calloways' IPS?":{"I and III":"A 4% raise is incremental change: worth monitoring, but not a reason to redesign the IPS.","II and III":"A one-week 2% dip is market noise, which the IPS is designed to help clients ignore.","I, II, and III":"Only the layoff is a discontinuous change; the raise is incremental and the dip is noise."},"Which approach best reconciles the spouses' preferences for their retirement assets?":{"Tactical asset allocation applied to the entire portfolio":"TAA shifts the whole mix on market views, with high turnover and costs, the opposite of Graham&rsquo;s low-cost, low-trading wish.","A single moderate model portfolio from a fund sponsor&rsquo;s questionnaire":"A sponsor model is a one-size-fits-all black box that ignores Nadia&rsquo;s specific wish for an active sleeve.","A strategic allocation held entirely in index funds with no active component":"An all-index portfolio satisfies Graham but gives Nadia no active satellite at all."},"Which strategy best meets Nadia's goals for her Kestrel shares?":{"Transfer the shares to a charitable remainder trust":"A CRT can sell without tax and creates a deduction, but the remainder goes to charity, contrary to her wish to keep the value in the family.","Give the shares to Leah and Sam":"A gift removes the shares from her control and may raise gift-tax issues; it moves the concentration rather than diversifying it.","Sell the shares and buy a diversified index fund":"Selling diversifies but triggers capital gains tax right away on a $200,000 gain (4,000 &times; $50)."},"Ignoring commissions, what is the minimum net value of Nadia's hedged KDX position at expiration (the value the puts lock in, less the premium paid)?":{"$300,000":"This subtracts the premium twice (as if the floor were $75 a share); the floor is $80 &times; 4,000 less the $10,000 premium once.","$320,000":"$320,000 is the strike value but ignores the $10,000 paid for the puts.","$350,000":"This subtracts the premium from today&rsquo;s value, not from the $80 strike floor."},"Which option position should Nadia add to her 4,000 KDX shares?":{"Write 40 $105 covered calls and nothing else":"Covered calls earn income and cap the upside, but the premium gives only a small cushion if KDX falls sharply.","Write 40 $80 puts and buy 40 $105 calls":"Writing puts adds a duty to buy more KDX if it falls, and buying calls adds upside exposure: the reverse of a hedge.","Buy 40 $90 calls and 40 $90 puts (a long straddle)":"A long straddle bets on a big move either way and costs two premiums; it is not a no-cost floor."},"Using the CAPM, what is the required return on KDX, and what does it imply about adding to the position?":{"18.0%; KDX is undervalued, so buy more":"18.0% multiplies the whole market return by beta (4% + 10% &times; 1.4) instead of only the market risk premium.","8.4%; the expected return exceeds it, so add to KDX":"8.4% is only the stock&rsquo;s risk premium (6% &times; 1.4); the risk-free rate must be added back.","14.0%; KDX plots on the security market line, so hold it unchanged":"14.0% is market return &times; beta with no risk-free intercept, and an asset on the SML would have expected return equal to required return, which is not the case."},"What is the weighted beta of the equity holdings in the joint brokerage account?":{"1.15":"1.15 is the simple average of the three betas; it ignores that KDX is 75% of the dollars.","1.40":"1.40 is KDX&rsquo;s beta alone; the two funds pull the weighted figure down.","1.07":"1.07 weights only the two funds and leaves out the KDX shares."},"Using the R² threshold taught in the course, which fund is the effective diversifier for Graham's account?":{"Large-Cap Value Index, R² = 0.88":"An R² of 0.88 means 88% of its movement tracks the S&amp;P 500; it largely duplicates the index fund.","Mid-Cap Blend, R² = 0.79":"0.79 is above the 70% threshold, so it moves too closely with the S&amp;P 500 to diversify well.","Dividend Growth, R² = 0.74":"0.74 is still above 70%; a correlation of 0.86 exceeds the roughly 0.84 cutoff."},"Which fund shows the better risk-adjusted performance, using the appropriate measure?":{"Bexley, because its Treynor ratio (7.50) beats Alder&rsquo;s (7.24)":"Treynor relies on beta, and Bexley&rsquo;s R² of 0.58 makes its beta unreliable, so its Treynor edge cannot be trusted.","Bexley, because its return is 1.4 points higher than Alder&rsquo;s":"Raw return ignores risk; Bexley earned its extra return with far higher volatility.","Alder, because its higher R² shows it has the better manager":"A higher R² only means the fund tracks the market more closely; it is not a measure of risk-adjusted performance or skill."},"Which of these statements about Harborline are CORRECT? I. Its high turnover adds trading costs not included in the 1.25% expense ratio. II. Its high turnover can produce capital gains distributions taxable to the Calloways. III. The expense ratio already includes the fund's brokerage commissions. IV. The manager's departure is a reason to monitor the fund more closely.":{"I and III":"Statement III is false (the expense ratio excludes brokerage commissions), and this choice leaves out true statements II and IV.","II and IV":"II and IV are true, but so is I: trading costs from turnover sit outside the expense ratio.","I, II, III, and IV":"III is false; the expense ratio covers operating expenses, management fees and 12b-1 fees, not the fund&rsquo;s brokerage commissions."},"What loss can the Calloways recognize on the December sale, and what is their basis in the repurchased shares?":{"$15,000 loss; basis $82,000":"The loss cannot be recognized because the same fund was bought back within 30 days, and the new basis must absorb the disallowed loss.","$3,000 loss; basis $94,000":"$3,000 is the annual cap on deducting net capital losses (2026 edition figure); here the whole loss is postponed by the wash-sale rule.","$0 loss; basis $82,000":"The disallowed $15,000 is not forfeited; it is added to the new shares&rsquo; basis, making it $97,000."},"What is the municipal fund's taxable-equivalent yield for the Calloways, and which fund pays more after tax?":{"5.29%; the municipal fund":"5.29% uses only the federal rate; for an in-state bond exempt from both taxes, the state rate must be included.","5.71%; the municipal fund":"Adding the two brackets (37%) is the shortcut for taxpayers who do not itemize; the Calloways itemize.","5.57%; the corporate fund":"The TEY is right but the conclusion is wrong: 5.57% beats the corporate fund&rsquo;s 5.2%, so the municipal fund wins after tax."},"Which change makes the Calloways' asset placement most tax-efficient while keeping their overall stock/bond mix?":{"Buy municipal bonds inside Nadia&rsquo;s Roth IRA so the bond income is doubly tax-free":"Municipal interest is already tax-exempt, so holding munis in a Roth wastes the account&rsquo;s shelter on income that needed none.","Keep the bond fund in the joint account, since its interest is taxed the same in any account":"Interest in a taxable account is taxed every year; inside a 401(k) it is deferred, so location matters.","Swap the bond fund for taxable zero-coupon corporate bonds in the joint account to defer the interest":"Taxable zero-coupon bonds create phantom income (accrued interest taxed yearly), so they also belong in tax-deferred accounts."},"Using the duration formula, approximately what will the Calloways' Ridgeway position be worth after the rate increase?":{"$56,280":"This uses the rule of thumb (6.2 &times; 1%) and skips dividing by 1 + YTM, which the formula requires.","$63,536":"The sign is backward; bond prices fall when rates rise.","$59,400":"This drops the value by only the 1% rate change and ignores the duration multiplier."},"Which fixed-income approach best protects the money for Leah's first tuition bill?":{"A long-term bond fund with a duration of 14, for its higher yield":"A duration of 14 is far longer than the 3-year horizon, so a rise in rates before tuition is due could cut the value sharply.","A barbell of 1-year and 20-year bonds":"A barbell needs active management and its long end carries large price risk; it does not lock in a value on a set date.","Keep the stock index fund, since stocks beat bonds over long periods":"With only three years left, a bear market could shrink the fund just before it is needed; near-term goals belong outside stocks."},"What was the Calloways' holding period return on the Summit fund in U.S. dollars?":{"9.00%":"9% is the return in euros; it ignores that each euro now converts into fewer dollars than at purchase.","15.41%":"15.41% inverts the exchange-rate change, treating the dollar&rsquo;s rise as a gain for a U.S. holder of euro assets.","3.44%":"3.44% subtracts the currency loss from the local return instead of compounding the two (1.09 &times; 0.9444 &minus; 1)."}});
